WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.458
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:15.810
HAGEGE: According to a
widespread assumption,

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:19.200
the topic on which I'm going
to deliver a talk today

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:23.140
does not belong in
linguistics proper.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:29.430
As a matter of fact,
giving this talk

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:34.050
here, in the MIT environment,
is not quite irrelevant.

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:35.100
Why?

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.560
Because this is the
place from which,

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:42.000
four or five years ago, a
new trend in linguistics

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.000
originated, the MIT as you know.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.780
As you may know, among the
tenants of this new paradigm

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:51.540
was the idea that language
is a phenomenon whose

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:55.200
study is part of the
cognitive sciences

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:58.080
and ultimately of biology.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:00.780
I am not among those who
work within this theoretical

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:04.709
framework, which appeared
in linguistics in the middle

00:01:04.709 --> 00:01:06.480
of the 20th century.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:09.480
Therefore, I do not think
that languages can fruitfully

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:14.220
be studied, only in cognitive
thought of being studied also

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.180
in--

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:19.710
let alone in biological terms,
short of being studied also

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:22.470
in sociological terms.

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:25.710
To me, language is
also, to a great extent,

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:27.300
is social phenomenon.

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:30.160
We also need social linguistics.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:32.790
Thus, I am going to
treat here language

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:36.120
death as a social-linguistic
phenomenon.

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:41.080
This talk takes most of
its ideas from this book.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:42.870
Where did I put it?

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:45.060
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:50.940
Whose English translation will
appear, as far I am informed,

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:53.940
in contrary to what
she announced--

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:55.860
she was not supposed
to know it--

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:58.860
will appear in Stanford
University Press

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:04.329
within, hopefully, one year or
probably, I am afraid, more.

00:02:04.329 --> 00:02:05.620
Under the title-- I don't know.

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:09.419
[INAUDIBLE] title I suggested
halting language death.

00:02:09.419 --> 00:02:12.240
But this was frowned
upon as quite un-English.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.240
Well, so anyway, it will
appear in an English version

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.000
within more or
less than one year

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:23.520
so that, right now, I am drawing
the main of my inspirations

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:25.680
and ideas from the
French version, which

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:27.360
I wrote two years ago.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:33.360
I would also like to mention
the name of my friend

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:39.730
and colleague, Ken Hale, whose
untimely death two weeks ago,

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:41.267
three weeks ago--

00:02:41.267 --> 00:02:41.850
AUDIENCE: One.

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:43.650
HAGEGE: One week ago
is a terrible loss

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:46.410
for us professional linguists.

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:48.990
Language death happens
to be-- turns out

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:51.240
to be also one of
the many topics which

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:53.610
is studied very brightly.

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:58.960
So he will be mourned and
this great loss for us.

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:01.230
All the more since,
as you know, this

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:04.060
was the place where he taught.

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:07.890
OK, let me announce
the plan I will

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:11.160
try to follow during much
more than 40 minutes.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:14.040
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:17.550
First, the state of the art,
the situation of languages,

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:20.670
as being on the brink of
extinction for most of them;

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:22.500
then, the causes
of this phenomenon;

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:28.320
then, the results of these
causes; then, the attempts

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:32.940
at a sudden revival attempt to
revive some languages according

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:37.210
to processes in which I will try
to give you some indications;

00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:42.440
and then, some exceptions,
which can be studied

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:46.610
in the framework of a so-called
Resurrection, which has

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:48.560
been announced in the title.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:53.450
So state of the art, what is the
situation today under our eyes?

00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:55.220
Oh, very simple.

00:03:55.220 --> 00:04:01.460
[INAUDIBLE] presented 25
languages die every year.

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:10.130
And the result of this cool
calculation is that in 2100,

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:11.750
considering that we have--

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:14.210
this is a strict assessment--

00:04:14.210 --> 00:04:17.070
5,000 languages, without taking
into account their dialects.

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:18.140
I mean, this is--

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:19.760
it's probably more.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:24.860
Considering this number of 5,000
languages, since 25 of them

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:30.920
die every year, in 2100, we
will have 2,500 languages.

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:35.620
Which for me, a language lover
since childhood, is a tragedy.

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:38.444
Of course, some
of you, probably,

00:04:38.444 --> 00:04:40.360
and some of my colleagues,
even, although they

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:44.720
are professional linguists like
me and many people in the mass

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:45.920
don't care.

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:48.230
This does not prevent
them from sleeping very--

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:49.580
quite fully.

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:51.500
It does, as far
as I am concerned,

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:53.420
and I will explain why.

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:59.390
We could consider the
situation normal, but I do not.

00:04:59.390 --> 00:05:02.040
You probably know that
in the 19th century,

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:08.030
there used to be what we can
call a vitalist tradition,

00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:13.160
V-I-T-A-L-I-S-T, which
consisted of studying languages

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:14.930
in biological terms.

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:18.900
And since the notion of death,
just like the notion of life,

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:22.340
are biological notions
applied to living species.

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:25.940
They were born, they live,
and then they disappear.

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:27.430
Not quite, not
completely, but they

00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:30.560
disappear as entities,
which are succeeded

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:34.650
by new entities, which
they produced or generated.

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:38.660
So this formulation, language
death, language life,

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:40.740
the life of language,
the title of many books

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:45.290
in linguistics in this century
and also in the 19th century,

00:05:45.290 --> 00:05:47.810
can be frowned
upon as metaphoric.

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:52.310
But they are not
quite unscientific.

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:55.920
They correspond to a
certain part of the truth.

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:59.690
So speaking of
language death, though

00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:03.470
metaphoric to a large extent,
is not quite to be ruled out.

00:06:03.470 --> 00:06:05.460
And what does it mean?

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:08.030
What is the lifespan of a
language, of human language?

00:06:08.030 --> 00:06:14.450
It begins when it appears,
then it leaves for some time

00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:18.500
according to the circumstances
and the environment in which it

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:19.430
appears.

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:21.920
And then, it
generally disappears.

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:25.360
However, these
properties which allow

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:28.360
us to study languages
in more or less

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:34.870
metaphorical biological
terms are not, all of them,

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:36.940
particular features
of languages.

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:39.240
There is one which does not--

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:43.840
which has never been mentioned
as far as living species are

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:48.730
concerned and which must be
mentioned as far as language

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:51.490
are concerned, and
this is the possibility

00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:53.530
not to die completely.

00:06:53.530 --> 00:06:57.040
Languages can be resurrected.

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:00.970
And this is what I will
try, if she leaves me time

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:07.330
for that, to refer to
at the end of this talk.

00:07:07.330 --> 00:07:10.540
Well, OK.

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:18.990
When languages
appear, their life,

00:07:18.990 --> 00:07:22.050
the lifespan, the
way they develop,

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:23.970
has many features,
which, as I said,

00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:28.380
are common with other
species, since I

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:32.880
accept this metaphorical
formulation, this wording.

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:35.280
Like other species,
living species,

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:36.920
of zoology, of
biology, of botanics,

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:40.700
and of various sub-branches,
etymology, and so on,

00:07:40.700 --> 00:07:43.530
the study of insects,
of other living species.

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:50.820
And to that extent, languages
can be characterized as species

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:55.020
which live and which change.

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:57.930
When do we think languages
should not be confused

00:07:57.930 --> 00:07:59.440
with language as a faculty?

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:01.360
Language as a faculty,
as you may know,

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:04.860
is a defining property
of our species.

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:11.430
Human species is characterized,
if not defined, practically,

00:08:11.430 --> 00:08:16.800
exclusively, by possessing,
in its genetic code, what

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:21.900
Chomsky himself here refers
to, using the French terms,

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:25.110
as [FRENCH], language faculty.

00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:27.780
This [FRENCH],, which was used
by the French philosopher,

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:30.420
Descartes, whom
Chomsky knew as well,

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:37.020
and to whom he devoted one
whole book, is what defines us.

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:44.652
This means that in minus
two millions 2 thousand

00:08:44.652 --> 00:08:47.970
of hundred years,
which is reputed

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:52.050
to be the probable date
when our species appears

00:08:52.050 --> 00:08:57.150
in a particular niche in
East Africa, as you may know.

00:08:57.150 --> 00:08:59.490
Since this time, minus--

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:04.500
before the Christian
era, we have language.

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:07.530
It defines us because it
appears in our genetic codes.

00:09:07.530 --> 00:09:10.050
But languages, English,
which has the same word,

00:09:10.050 --> 00:09:14.040
does not permit us to distinct
distinguish, as French does,

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:17.400
which says [FRENCH].

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:20.250
So [FRENCH],, are
not the same term.

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:24.030
English is forced to use either
the singular or the plural

00:09:24.030 --> 00:09:26.430
in order to refer to
these two notions.

00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:31.290
And language, as a defining
property of human species,

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:34.560
is something which
does not necessarily

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:37.110
result in languages at once.

00:09:37.110 --> 00:09:39.360
So although this is
quite hypothetical,

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:43.200
I suppose, I think,
languages in the plural

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:46.410
appeared much later
than the species itself.

00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:50.370
But as soon as the species
appears, the only thing which

00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:54.480
characterizes it as distinct
from other-- we are a species.

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:56.040
We are an animal
species, of course.

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:57.748
But the only thing
which distinguishes us

00:09:57.748 --> 00:10:02.100
from other animal species is
the inscription of language

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:04.050
in our genetic code.

00:10:04.050 --> 00:10:07.770
And the last apes,
which are still

00:10:07.770 --> 00:10:12.000
apes, and the first hominids,
which are really humans,

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:16.020
are distinguished not by
their capacity or their skull,

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:20.910
not by measures of their
morphological being,

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:24.480
but exclusively by the
possession or not possession

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:26.730
of the fact that they
possess or do not

00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:31.320
possess this faculty,
language, in their code.

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:37.410
Well, later, much later, I
mean, close to our period,

00:10:37.410 --> 00:10:42.450
probably end of
the Neolithic era,

00:10:42.450 --> 00:10:48.510
minus 10-12,000 before
Christ, we probably had--

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:51.810
this is a hypothesis, but it
is probably propped by many

00:10:51.810 --> 00:10:55.260
arguments, which I cannot
mention here, for lack of time,

00:10:55.260 --> 00:10:56.130
for lack of time.

00:10:56.130 --> 00:10:58.020
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:58.020 --> 00:11:03.780
It was the period in which
many languages appeared.

00:11:03.780 --> 00:11:06.480
And the reason is
not far to seek.

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:10.500
Why do so many languages
appear in the beginning

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:11.880
of the Neolithic?

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:17.100
Just because this is the period
when human societies become

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:18.270
societies.

00:11:18.270 --> 00:11:21.180
In fact, by becoming
sedentaries,

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:23.220
they were nomadizing so far.

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:25.800
And approximately
at that period,

00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:29.190
they begin to build cities.

00:11:29.190 --> 00:11:34.470
And building cities
means to become peasants,

00:11:34.470 --> 00:11:39.180
to have an activity, which is
agriculture, and also to seize,

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:41.100
to stop, to a large
extent, without completely

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:45.120
stopping being what we
call hunter-gatherer.

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:47.670
A hunter-gatherer
is a characteristic,

00:11:47.670 --> 00:11:52.260
still represented in Australia
in some parts of New Guinea

00:11:52.260 --> 00:11:54.150
and other parts of
the world, which

00:11:54.150 --> 00:11:58.920
characterizes very old
societies, the first ones.

00:11:58.920 --> 00:12:00.030
I do not--

00:12:00.030 --> 00:12:03.870
I am reluctant-- I am loath
to call them primitive.

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:06.890
Of course, as so many
people do, because this

00:12:06.890 --> 00:12:11.550
would be a value judgment
against which I am completely--

00:12:11.550 --> 00:12:13.860
but I would just say,
that the societies which

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:15.930
are, which were,
which used to be,

00:12:15.930 --> 00:12:19.490
and which, for some of
them, still existing are yet

00:12:19.490 --> 00:12:23.350
still hunter-gatherers
are of course nomads,

00:12:23.350 --> 00:12:25.100
nomadizing societies.

00:12:25.100 --> 00:12:31.010
Because they just live from what
they hunt and what they gather.

00:12:31.010 --> 00:12:34.630
Gathering-- a hunter-gatherer
implies fishing.

00:12:34.630 --> 00:12:36.110
Or when you hunt,
you don't fish.

00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:39.890
But fishing is just put
together with hunting

00:12:39.890 --> 00:12:41.960
as a means of subsistence.

00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:47.240
And these societies were
the most ancient we know.

00:12:47.240 --> 00:12:52.730
As soon as they become sedentary
and stop to some extent

00:12:52.730 --> 00:12:56.540
and for some of them,
completely, being nomads,

00:12:56.540 --> 00:13:00.290
stop nomadizing, then
they build cities.

00:13:00.290 --> 00:13:02.210
We can't say without
contradiction,

00:13:02.210 --> 00:13:05.210
without provocation,
that building cities is

00:13:05.210 --> 00:13:06.980
the same as becoming peasants.

00:13:06.980 --> 00:13:09.710
Because when you build cities,
you build them in the lands.

00:13:09.710 --> 00:13:13.490
So the French humorist,
Alfonso Allais, said, [FRENCH]..

00:13:16.170 --> 00:13:17.612
But of course, this is not.

00:13:17.612 --> 00:13:18.570
This was a provocation.

00:13:18.570 --> 00:13:22.340
But in fact, where can we build
cities if not in the land?

00:13:22.340 --> 00:13:25.190
I mean, within the landscape.

00:13:25.190 --> 00:13:26.750
And then when we build cities.

00:13:26.750 --> 00:13:28.730
The ones which are
cited by the Bible,

00:13:28.730 --> 00:13:32.690
like Jericho, reputed
to be, probably wrongly,

00:13:32.690 --> 00:13:35.730
one of the most ancient
one, it remotes to--

00:13:35.730 --> 00:13:39.650
it goes back to a much
remote antiquity, but not

00:13:39.650 --> 00:13:40.730
the most remote one.

00:13:40.730 --> 00:13:44.480
I mean, probably 9,000
years before the beginning

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:46.520
of the Christian era.

00:13:46.520 --> 00:13:52.370
Anyway, whatever the first known
city, Jericho or any other one,

00:13:52.370 --> 00:13:56.900
building these cities implies
necessarily that people--

00:13:56.900 --> 00:14:00.980
these societies were
becoming agricultures.

00:14:00.980 --> 00:14:05.720
And therefore, they
seized nomadizing.

00:14:05.720 --> 00:14:09.740
And due to the complications
of their relationships,

00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:14.250
they multiplied the
number of their languages.

00:14:14.250 --> 00:14:18.980
So we are quite sure, very sure,
that the number of languages

00:14:18.980 --> 00:14:23.540
grew considerably when
human societies became

00:14:23.540 --> 00:14:26.340
sedentary societies.

00:14:26.340 --> 00:14:31.790
This implying that relationships
are more and more complex when

00:14:31.790 --> 00:14:33.170
societies--

00:14:33.170 --> 00:14:37.940
once they are established,
settled in a place,

00:14:37.940 --> 00:14:40.340
have new kinds of
relationships which

00:14:40.340 --> 00:14:44.720
force them to build new kinds
of conception of the worlds

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:46.790
and to study it
and to express it

00:14:46.790 --> 00:14:50.270
with a much more
refined communication

00:14:50.270 --> 00:14:52.610
means, which is language.

00:14:52.610 --> 00:14:58.100
Well, this concerns
the Neolithic.

00:14:58.100 --> 00:15:03.410
But then after this
birth and development,

00:15:03.410 --> 00:15:06.690
at a period which is
very close to ours,

00:15:06.690 --> 00:15:10.401
a Renaissance or beginning
of the 16th century,

00:15:10.401 --> 00:15:12.650
there was a sharp decrease
in the number of languages.

00:15:12.650 --> 00:15:17.120
And this is essentially
due to the discovery

00:15:17.120 --> 00:15:22.710
of new worlds, which is a
warlike, a hawkish discovery.

00:15:22.710 --> 00:15:25.700
People do not
discover a new world

00:15:25.700 --> 00:15:27.980
just by observing
its characteristics.

00:15:27.980 --> 00:15:29.610
They murder other people.

00:15:29.610 --> 00:15:33.200
And this is one of the first
causes of language death.

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:35.840
They lead them to the
brink of extinction.

00:15:35.840 --> 00:15:39.350
I mean both ethnically
and linguistically.

00:15:39.350 --> 00:15:47.310
The ones which escape Western
violence lose the languages.

00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:50.400
And the ones who do
not lose their life

00:15:50.400 --> 00:15:52.900
and in the same time, the
languages and their cultures.

00:15:52.900 --> 00:15:56.940
So we know that the
number of languages

00:15:56.940 --> 00:16:00.810
is submitted to a sharp
decrease in the beginning

00:16:00.810 --> 00:16:03.300
of the 15th century,
which is, by the way,

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:08.640
the beginning of what
are called the most

00:16:08.640 --> 00:16:10.380
beautiful discoveries
of the world

00:16:10.380 --> 00:16:13.290
by, most of the
time, Westerners.

00:16:13.290 --> 00:16:19.620
Well, this implies
that in America,

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:21.270
three parts of
America, in Australia

00:16:21.270 --> 00:16:25.260
later, in New Zealand,
in Russia also,

00:16:25.260 --> 00:16:30.090
languages after having
thrived, after having

00:16:30.090 --> 00:16:33.390
been extremely numerous,
become decreasing

00:16:33.390 --> 00:16:35.850
at very rapid speeds.

00:16:35.850 --> 00:16:43.810
And in Africa, Nigeria,
Tanzania, Kenya

00:16:43.810 --> 00:16:47.290
we also see many, many
languages disappearing for

00:16:47.290 --> 00:16:49.480
causes which I'm
going to study now.

00:16:49.480 --> 00:16:54.040
So I have just to begin with--

00:16:54.040 --> 00:16:56.620
presented a situation where
I call a state of the art.

00:16:56.620 --> 00:17:01.180
And now, let me
propose some causes.

00:17:01.180 --> 00:17:04.450
The first cause, which is
quite easy to understand,

00:17:04.450 --> 00:17:07.000
is, as I said,
physical extinction.

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:09.280
Of course, when
there is slaughter,

00:17:09.280 --> 00:17:13.990
when you kill people,
they disappear

00:17:13.990 --> 00:17:16.520
and their languages
disappear at the same time.

00:17:16.520 --> 00:17:21.849
So this is the history of
the great part of the world.

00:17:21.849 --> 00:17:28.170
We can say that the discovery
of North, Central, and South

00:17:28.170 --> 00:17:33.840
America is the discovery
of continents in which

00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:37.620
Europeans are not mingling most
of the time, with an exception.

00:17:37.620 --> 00:17:42.090
The Spaniards mingled much more
regionally than the Anglo-Saxon

00:17:42.090 --> 00:17:45.212
Puritans with the Indians.

00:17:45.212 --> 00:17:46.420
Of course, they needed women.

00:17:46.420 --> 00:17:51.330
So not mingling in general
with the native populations,

00:17:51.330 --> 00:17:54.660
but pushing them further
away from their settlements

00:17:54.660 --> 00:17:59.100
or killing them more by the
spread of old world diseases

00:17:59.100 --> 00:18:03.060
than in wards,
led their language

00:18:03.060 --> 00:18:04.630
and themselves to extinction.

00:18:04.630 --> 00:18:08.610
This is one of the chief causes.

00:18:08.610 --> 00:18:12.150
Now we also have social
and professional causes.

00:18:12.150 --> 00:18:18.180
I mean, when the language is
presented as providing new jobs

00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:24.030
or as being the vector of new
values, social values, even

00:18:24.030 --> 00:18:28.260
cultural values, which
correspond to enrichment

00:18:28.260 --> 00:18:31.500
and the possibility to
affirm oneself more readily,

00:18:31.500 --> 00:18:36.720
then people switch from
their ancestral language,

00:18:36.720 --> 00:18:39.120
from their vernacular
language, to this new language,

00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:44.990
which is imputed to bring
a new kind of living

00:18:44.990 --> 00:18:46.740
judged preferable.

00:18:46.740 --> 00:18:49.410
So this is what
happens in many places

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:52.810
and especially in
America and in Africa.

00:18:52.810 --> 00:18:58.200
So the second cause
of language extinction

00:18:58.200 --> 00:19:00.120
after physical
extinction itself is

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:02.710
social and professional
changes under a contact.

00:19:05.760 --> 00:19:09.780
In Africa, for
example, this example

00:19:09.780 --> 00:19:14.190
will illustrate the
way languages are

00:19:14.190 --> 00:19:17.250
led to the brink of extinction,
not necessarily by Westerners

00:19:17.250 --> 00:19:20.100
but also by Africans themselves
in their relationship

00:19:20.100 --> 00:19:22.440
with other Africans.

00:19:22.440 --> 00:19:26.585
When-- if I am a nomad, I am a--

00:19:26.585 --> 00:19:33.000
I have nothing to live off,
and I just hunt and gather.

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:38.040
And I marry a woman
whose father has cattle.

00:19:38.040 --> 00:19:42.370
Since I buy her-- women
are bought, of course.

00:19:42.370 --> 00:19:45.420
In these societies,
women are bought.

00:19:45.420 --> 00:19:50.880
And since her father,
in order to negotiate

00:19:50.880 --> 00:19:53.730
this commercial act
with me, gives me

00:19:53.730 --> 00:19:56.150
some heads of
cattle, which he has,

00:19:56.150 --> 00:20:00.480
if she belongs to a family
in which people are pastors,

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:03.000
then I change my way of life.

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:06.540
And from this normadizing
life, which I had formerly,

00:20:06.540 --> 00:20:11.550
to the new life which is
brought to me by my marriage,

00:20:11.550 --> 00:20:14.660
I become a new kind of man.

00:20:14.660 --> 00:20:19.170
That means that I give up
my former nomadizing life.

00:20:19.170 --> 00:20:23.730
Then, the question which
appears as the main one

00:20:23.730 --> 00:20:28.290
is, what language will be
transmitted to the children?

00:20:28.290 --> 00:20:31.440
Of course, the woman,
whatever kind of feelings

00:20:31.440 --> 00:20:35.100
she has for her husband,
will not learn his language.

00:20:35.100 --> 00:20:36.000
What will she do?

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:39.340
She will transmit the
language of her father,

00:20:39.340 --> 00:20:41.850
the one of her
tribe, which is hers,

00:20:41.850 --> 00:20:44.220
and then the children
will be brought up

00:20:44.220 --> 00:20:46.050
in the language of the mother.

00:20:46.050 --> 00:20:50.790
And this is one of the main
social and professional causes

00:20:50.790 --> 00:20:53.500
of the disappearing
of languages.

00:20:53.500 --> 00:20:56.230
So marriage is a very
dangerous thing for languages.

00:20:56.230 --> 00:20:56.730
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:56.730 --> 00:20:57.295
It depends.

00:20:57.295 --> 00:20:58.670
It depends what
kind of marriage.

00:20:58.670 --> 00:21:03.900
But in that particular
case, it's, unfortunately,

00:21:03.900 --> 00:21:05.735
totally counter-indicated.

00:21:10.690 --> 00:21:18.340
Another cause is the models
provided by urban life.

00:21:18.340 --> 00:21:20.110
Therefore, the
previous one which

00:21:20.110 --> 00:21:24.070
I was referring to
just some minutes ago,

00:21:24.070 --> 00:21:27.970
I will illustrate probably
by the example of Kwegu, who

00:21:27.970 --> 00:21:31.240
are a nomadizing population--
who were, used to be formerly,

00:21:31.240 --> 00:21:35.320
but they have changed
their way of life in Kenya.

00:21:35.320 --> 00:21:38.290
And most of them, by these
kind of marriages to which I

00:21:38.290 --> 00:21:42.250
was referring, become pastors.

00:21:42.250 --> 00:21:47.800
And in some kinds,
they are cultivators.

00:21:47.800 --> 00:21:51.730
And then their language dies,
because they won't transmit it.

00:21:51.730 --> 00:21:54.670
And this is the
case of these Kwegu

00:21:54.670 --> 00:21:59.470
marrying Maasai women in
Kenya and south of Tanzania.

00:21:59.470 --> 00:22:04.450
As far as the other cause
is concerned to which--

00:22:04.450 --> 00:22:11.740
models which are provided by
a new urban life, a case which

00:22:11.740 --> 00:22:17.080
I could mention is the one
of the Nubians of high Egypt.

00:22:17.080 --> 00:22:20.260
These people belong
to an ethni which

00:22:20.260 --> 00:22:25.210
has a Nilo-Saharan language,
Nubian, well studied

00:22:25.210 --> 00:22:26.170
by linguists.

00:22:26.170 --> 00:22:30.230
Myself, I did field work on
this language some years ago.

00:22:30.230 --> 00:22:33.510
And they are Muslims.

00:22:33.510 --> 00:22:39.430
And since they are Muslims,
they are very regularly

00:22:39.430 --> 00:22:45.760
submitted to the religious
emissions of broadcasting's

00:22:45.760 --> 00:22:49.040
of Cairo in Arabic.

00:22:49.040 --> 00:22:50.740
So they become bilinguals.

00:22:50.740 --> 00:22:53.710
They speak their Nubian,
vernacular language,

00:22:53.710 --> 00:22:58.960
and they also speak Arabic,
which is the vector of Islam,

00:22:58.960 --> 00:23:03.560
and which they catch
when they listen or watch

00:23:03.560 --> 00:23:07.340
their broadcasting and
television programs in Arabic.

00:23:07.340 --> 00:23:09.940
So this they do
in urban centers.

00:23:09.940 --> 00:23:11.950
Because in their
countries, it is

00:23:11.950 --> 00:23:14.720
difficult to catch these media.

00:23:14.720 --> 00:23:18.910
But when they go to Cairo or to
other important cities of Egypt

00:23:18.910 --> 00:23:22.420
and Sudan, then
they progressively

00:23:22.420 --> 00:23:25.370
abandon, give up
their own language.

00:23:25.370 --> 00:23:29.140
And this is the way by
which life in urban centers

00:23:29.140 --> 00:23:35.710
is threatening-- is a very
big threat for the maintaining

00:23:35.710 --> 00:23:36.560
of languages.

00:23:36.560 --> 00:23:40.180
So this is the way
most of them die.

00:23:40.180 --> 00:23:42.580
Another one, which I
would like to refer to,

00:23:42.580 --> 00:23:46.270
although it does not
belong as the previous ones

00:23:46.270 --> 00:23:49.870
to those which can be
studied scientifically.

00:23:49.870 --> 00:23:52.270
I mean using
scientific concepts,

00:23:52.270 --> 00:23:59.140
such as professional life,
change of lifestyle, marriage,

00:23:59.140 --> 00:24:04.840
and so on, is a reason
which I am, to some extent,

00:24:04.840 --> 00:24:06.370
hesitant to mention.

00:24:06.370 --> 00:24:11.860
But I don't see any other
reason for the extinction

00:24:11.860 --> 00:24:15.920
of many languages in the modern
world that what I would call--

00:24:15.920 --> 00:24:18.370
I would refer to as snobbishry.

00:24:18.370 --> 00:24:21.610
In many cases, the adoption
of English, precisely,

00:24:21.610 --> 00:24:25.240
by populations which
do not need it.

00:24:25.240 --> 00:24:28.720
Neither in their social life nor
in their professional life nor

00:24:28.720 --> 00:24:31.885
for commercial or
economic reasons.

00:24:31.885 --> 00:24:35.680
Is not explainable
short of referring

00:24:35.680 --> 00:24:38.170
to reasons of snobbishry.

00:24:38.170 --> 00:24:40.900
This is the case in
many European countries

00:24:40.900 --> 00:24:46.030
in which English is
promoted not by the pressure

00:24:46.030 --> 00:24:47.920
of British or
American authorities,

00:24:47.920 --> 00:24:53.510
not by people who are
themselves bearers, I would say,

00:24:53.510 --> 00:24:57.340
or speakers of English,
but by the very populations

00:24:57.340 --> 00:25:01.030
of European countries, Western
countries in Europe who

00:25:01.030 --> 00:25:04.070
consider, which is by no
means my case of course,

00:25:04.070 --> 00:25:09.900
that speaking English is
something which is prestigious.

00:25:09.900 --> 00:25:12.180
I cannot share this ridiculous
attitude, of course,

00:25:12.180 --> 00:25:15.880
but it is the one
we see in many,

00:25:15.880 --> 00:25:20.360
many Western milieu and
especially in the bourgeoisie

00:25:20.360 --> 00:25:24.730
in France, Germany,
Spain, Italy, and so on.

00:25:24.730 --> 00:25:29.870
And the reason why I mention--
at the end of this sub-chapter

00:25:29.870 --> 00:25:31.830
on causes of
language extinction--

00:25:31.830 --> 00:25:35.670
the reason why I mention it
at the end is that snobbishry,

00:25:35.670 --> 00:25:42.720
as I said, cannot be proposed
as a scientific cause.

00:25:42.720 --> 00:25:44.160
But it is.

00:25:44.160 --> 00:25:48.660
At any rate, it is the only
one I see when I don't--

00:25:48.660 --> 00:25:50.490
when I can't
mention other causes

00:25:50.490 --> 00:25:54.060
to explain switching
from one language

00:25:54.060 --> 00:25:57.360
to another one which one
does not really need.

00:25:57.360 --> 00:26:02.820
As far as commerce and
profit, the only care

00:26:02.820 --> 00:26:05.760
of these kind of
people is concerned,

00:26:05.760 --> 00:26:08.850
selling or buying in English, as
they think they are forced to,

00:26:08.850 --> 00:26:13.680
does not, by any means,
improve their business

00:26:13.680 --> 00:26:19.930
nor leads them to a better
kind of invention or creativity

00:26:19.930 --> 00:26:20.430
and so on.

00:26:20.430 --> 00:26:24.510
But nevertheless, they think
that switching to English

00:26:24.510 --> 00:26:27.580
is something which
is to be praised.

00:26:27.580 --> 00:26:34.200
Well, now let me present the
results of such a situation.

00:26:34.200 --> 00:26:40.260
I will give some examples to
illustrate the set of causes

00:26:40.260 --> 00:26:42.870
and to show what these
causes have brought

00:26:42.870 --> 00:26:46.560
about as a result.
Let me tell you

00:26:46.560 --> 00:26:48.750
these examples in various
parts of the world.

00:26:48.750 --> 00:26:51.730
And let me begin
with an example,

00:26:51.730 --> 00:26:55.380
in the United States of
Norwegian, which you might--

00:26:55.380 --> 00:26:57.720
some of you might know,
probably some of you

00:26:57.720 --> 00:27:00.510
who have Norwegian background
have heard of that.

00:27:00.510 --> 00:27:06.720
After an initial migration
of 1,925 persons, very few,

00:27:06.720 --> 00:27:10.140
Norwegians emigrated to the
United States in such numbers

00:27:10.140 --> 00:27:13.380
that they were able to
create a Norwegian America.

00:27:13.380 --> 00:27:19.500
And this Norwegian America
flourished from 1850 to 1940.

00:27:19.500 --> 00:27:21.720
During most of this
period, the chief medium

00:27:21.720 --> 00:27:24.540
of internal communication,
oral and written,

00:27:24.540 --> 00:27:26.820
was the Norwegian
language, which served

00:27:26.820 --> 00:27:31.130
as an in-group identity marker.

00:27:31.130 --> 00:27:33.460
Widely spoken in
several parts of,

00:27:33.460 --> 00:27:38.830
as you know, of Minnesota,
Wisconsin, Ohio, Indiana, Iowa,

00:27:38.830 --> 00:27:42.520
South and North Dakota,
and some other states.

00:27:42.520 --> 00:27:45.630
Research on informant
usage shows that--

00:27:45.630 --> 00:27:48.010
I have myself done
some fieldwork

00:27:48.010 --> 00:27:50.410
in some of these states
which I have just mentioned--

00:27:50.410 --> 00:27:52.960
shows that in the more
conservative and isolated

00:27:52.960 --> 00:27:57.310
rural settlements the language
persisted well into the second,

00:27:57.310 --> 00:28:00.340
that is, the first
American generation

00:28:00.340 --> 00:28:04.420
as late as the 1940s,
which means very recently.

00:28:04.420 --> 00:28:05.920
One could still
find speakers who

00:28:05.920 --> 00:28:09.310
claimed to be using Norwegian
regularly in the United States

00:28:09.310 --> 00:28:10.800
at least one half of their time.

00:28:13.540 --> 00:28:16.090
However, among these
bilinguals, Norwegian

00:28:16.090 --> 00:28:19.780
became typically a family
or neighborhood language,

00:28:19.780 --> 00:28:23.230
gradually abandoned as contacts
with American institutions

00:28:23.230 --> 00:28:24.450
and individuals grew.

00:28:29.010 --> 00:28:32.040
Several informants reported
that they had first

00:28:32.040 --> 00:28:35.340
learned English at a
school after speaking

00:28:35.340 --> 00:28:37.300
only Norwegian in their home.

00:28:37.300 --> 00:28:39.800
The discipline
could be traumatic.

00:28:39.800 --> 00:28:42.930
An informant told me that
at the district school--

00:28:42.930 --> 00:28:45.090
I am mentioning what he said--

00:28:45.090 --> 00:28:48.290
we were not allowed
to speak Norwegian.

00:28:48.290 --> 00:28:51.440
Another puts it that,
when we were little,

00:28:51.440 --> 00:28:55.430
we could not talk Yankee, and
the teacher would keep us after

00:28:55.430 --> 00:28:58.000
school if we didn't--

00:28:58.000 --> 00:28:59.800
if we did.

00:28:59.800 --> 00:29:02.770
The school was, of course, not
alone in promoting English.

00:29:02.770 --> 00:29:05.540
Several mentioned contacts
with the Norwegian neighbors,

00:29:05.540 --> 00:29:08.500
especially Irish and
Germans, though there

00:29:08.500 --> 00:29:10.960
was an occasional German
who learned Norwegian

00:29:10.960 --> 00:29:15.140
from surroundings, are also to
be mentioned among the causes.

00:29:18.997 --> 00:29:27.330
After World War II,
Norwegian almost

00:29:27.330 --> 00:29:31.410
disappeared as it was as a
result of all the factors

00:29:31.410 --> 00:29:33.540
which I have mentioned.

00:29:33.540 --> 00:29:35.880
The scenario here
outlined does not

00:29:35.880 --> 00:29:37.500
seem to be very
different from that

00:29:37.500 --> 00:29:41.130
which applies to most immigrants
languages in the United States,

00:29:41.130 --> 00:29:44.520
and also in Canada, to
some extent in other parts

00:29:44.520 --> 00:29:49.560
of the world which are now, for
the majority, English speaking.

00:29:49.560 --> 00:29:52.020
There are special
profiles, however.

00:29:52.020 --> 00:29:55.140
For example, for Pennsylvania
German where immigration

00:29:55.140 --> 00:29:57.240
was early and massive.

00:29:57.240 --> 00:30:00.540
In a comparison, very small
modern groups, numbers,

00:30:00.540 --> 00:30:02.430
and religious
boundaries may lead

00:30:02.430 --> 00:30:05.880
to greater retentiveness,
as with a million Germans,

00:30:05.880 --> 00:30:09.120
or less, as in the
case of American Danes.

00:30:09.120 --> 00:30:11.610
English is not only the
proto-American language

00:30:11.610 --> 00:30:15.210
of choice, promoted by
official and private usage,

00:30:15.210 --> 00:30:19.200
it is also the lingua Franca
of all the earlier immigrants

00:30:19.200 --> 00:30:24.010
who are sooner or later driven
to communicate with each other.

00:30:24.010 --> 00:30:27.010
And this is the fate of
Norwegian in this country.

00:30:27.010 --> 00:30:29.910
Let me mention
another example which

00:30:29.910 --> 00:30:35.640
has to do with Castilianized
or Hispanized South and Central

00:30:35.640 --> 00:30:36.720
America.

00:30:36.720 --> 00:30:39.270
In these countries,
as you know, we

00:30:39.270 --> 00:30:42.390
had a very great number
of local languages,

00:30:42.390 --> 00:30:46.230
especially in Mexico,
Nahuatl in the north

00:30:46.230 --> 00:30:48.329
and the Mayan
languages in the South,

00:30:48.329 --> 00:30:50.370
as well as some other
ones, Chinantec, and so on,

00:30:50.370 --> 00:30:53.790
which can still be studied
by people doing field work,

00:30:53.790 --> 00:30:58.092
like myself, but which are
more or less, a great number

00:30:58.092 --> 00:30:59.550
of them, on the
brink of extinction

00:30:59.550 --> 00:31:01.170
today, unfortunately.

00:31:01.170 --> 00:31:04.770
These languages, these
people lost their languages

00:31:04.770 --> 00:31:07.470
and their cultures to
the Spanish culture,

00:31:07.470 --> 00:31:09.220
to the Spanish language.

00:31:09.220 --> 00:31:11.940
And this was, to a
large extent, due

00:31:11.940 --> 00:31:14.250
to evangelization
to Christianization

00:31:14.250 --> 00:31:18.870
and also to the fact that
just the same colonial habit

00:31:18.870 --> 00:31:23.760
of which we see so many examples
all over the world, people

00:31:23.760 --> 00:31:27.660
when they colonize or when they
arrived in these countries,

00:31:27.660 --> 00:31:32.220
took the best lands, took
the best places to cultivate

00:31:32.220 --> 00:31:35.430
and pushed away
farther and farther

00:31:35.430 --> 00:31:38.400
the original inhabitants,
who of course

00:31:38.400 --> 00:31:41.550
either died because
they had nothing to eat

00:31:41.550 --> 00:31:48.570
or because they did not
develop and tie elements

00:31:48.570 --> 00:31:54.390
to defend themselves against the
diseases brought by Europeans.

00:31:54.390 --> 00:32:00.110
So what happens to their body,
happened to their languages.

00:32:00.110 --> 00:32:04.740
Another case is the
formation of new languages,

00:32:04.740 --> 00:32:08.840
which is not the same thing as
the one I am referring to now.

00:32:08.840 --> 00:32:13.640
I mean, either language
is imposed upon,

00:32:13.640 --> 00:32:18.530
foisted on people who are
deprived, by the same token,

00:32:18.530 --> 00:32:19.790
of their own language.

00:32:19.790 --> 00:32:22.720
And in that case, it
is a substitution.

00:32:22.720 --> 00:32:26.240
Or another way, another
process by which

00:32:26.240 --> 00:32:30.320
we can say that we
have language death,

00:32:30.320 --> 00:32:33.410
language splits into
other languages.

00:32:33.410 --> 00:32:36.200
And by splitting, it
becomes other languages

00:32:36.200 --> 00:32:40.280
which do not
necessarily replace it

00:32:40.280 --> 00:32:44.310
but are its outgrowth
or its products.

00:32:44.310 --> 00:32:46.760
This is exactly
what I am describing

00:32:46.760 --> 00:32:51.870
in my own, very poor terms,
what has happened to French,

00:32:51.870 --> 00:32:54.470
Italian, Spanish,
Romanian, and Portuguese,

00:32:54.470 --> 00:32:57.560
the so-called new Latin
or Roman languages, which

00:32:57.560 --> 00:33:04.460
are as you know, the results
of the splitting of Latin.

00:33:04.460 --> 00:33:09.100
Latin split into these
five, in fact, much more,

00:33:09.100 --> 00:33:12.860
but these five main
so-called Latin languages.

00:33:12.860 --> 00:33:15.800
In that case, will we
say that Latin is dead?

00:33:15.800 --> 00:33:17.660
This I will-- how long is left?

00:33:20.845 --> 00:33:21.760
AUDIENCE: 10 minutes.

00:33:21.760 --> 00:33:25.560
HAGEGE: Well, I'm
going to mention now,

00:33:25.560 --> 00:33:30.450
in the chapter which is
devoted to resurrection.

00:33:30.450 --> 00:33:32.920
I'm going to talk
about that now.

00:33:32.920 --> 00:33:35.890
Well, has Latin disappeared?

00:33:35.890 --> 00:33:39.720
No, to the extent that
Latin has given way

00:33:39.720 --> 00:33:45.990
and has been the source from
which other languages have

00:33:45.990 --> 00:33:47.340
developed.

00:33:47.340 --> 00:33:49.140
Yes, it has disappeared
to the extent

00:33:49.140 --> 00:33:52.730
that Spanish is not Latin,
French isn't either.

00:33:52.730 --> 00:33:56.100
In spite of that, I'd like to
call your attention to the fact

00:33:56.100 --> 00:33:58.500
that the reason for me--

00:33:58.500 --> 00:34:01.380
I have also delved
into books which

00:34:01.380 --> 00:34:03.720
confirm this
position-- the reason

00:34:03.720 --> 00:34:07.380
why America was called Latin,
why would America be Latin?

00:34:07.380 --> 00:34:10.409
Of course, we are so used
to such a designation

00:34:10.409 --> 00:34:12.600
that we do not wonder why.

00:34:12.600 --> 00:34:14.520
But why did the
Spaniards, when they

00:34:14.520 --> 00:34:18.810
invaded it, the conquistadors,
why did they call it Latin?

00:34:18.810 --> 00:34:22.350
The reason is that they consider
themselves bearers of Latin.

00:34:22.350 --> 00:34:25.920
In other words, in spite
of the fact that Castilian,

00:34:25.920 --> 00:34:29.550
the purest termination
of Spanish, was no more,

00:34:29.550 --> 00:34:32.580
was no longer, since
centuries and centuries Latin,

00:34:32.580 --> 00:34:35.400
they still believed--
they were still convinced

00:34:35.400 --> 00:34:38.027
that what they spoke was Latin.

00:34:38.027 --> 00:34:40.110
It had been transformed
to some extent, but Latin.

00:34:40.110 --> 00:34:45.530
And this is the way, the reason
why Hispanized America was

00:34:45.530 --> 00:34:49.889
called Latin America, although
it was not Latin by any means.

00:34:49.889 --> 00:34:53.650
But it is the way the
Spaniards viewed it.

00:34:53.650 --> 00:34:59.210
Well, now in other
cases, languages

00:34:59.210 --> 00:35:04.340
try to defend themselves against
the assaults of languages

00:35:04.340 --> 00:35:11.040
of invaders and to resist
and to try to avoid dying.

00:35:11.040 --> 00:35:14.060
Well, now let me--

00:35:14.060 --> 00:35:18.200
as one of the last
parts of this talk--

00:35:18.200 --> 00:35:24.260
let me, as I announced, say
what I think about the attempts

00:35:24.260 --> 00:35:25.889
to revive some languages.

00:35:25.889 --> 00:35:27.680
There are some attempts
to revive languages

00:35:27.680 --> 00:35:29.330
and some of them are successful.

00:35:29.330 --> 00:35:30.290
Some are less.

00:35:30.290 --> 00:35:32.390
Some are not at all.

00:35:32.390 --> 00:35:35.680
What are these attempts?

00:35:35.680 --> 00:35:40.870
Many people, mostly
missionaries and linguists,

00:35:40.870 --> 00:35:43.450
do not satisfy themselves
with the samples

00:35:43.450 --> 00:35:47.680
taken that language is being
extinct and progressively

00:35:47.680 --> 00:35:48.370
dying.

00:35:48.370 --> 00:35:53.860
They try to revive them and in
some cases, against the will

00:35:53.860 --> 00:35:55.870
of the population themselves.

00:35:55.870 --> 00:35:58.000
I mean, to the extent
that, as I said

00:35:58.000 --> 00:36:00.430
in the beginning of
this talk, populations,

00:36:00.430 --> 00:36:03.520
for various causes
which I referred to,

00:36:03.520 --> 00:36:06.760
consider that they do not have
to translate their vernacular

00:36:06.760 --> 00:36:09.340
languages to their
descendants or their children.

00:36:09.340 --> 00:36:12.520
To that extent, when a linguist
or a missionary, a missionary

00:36:12.520 --> 00:36:15.790
who translate the Bible
in order to evangelize

00:36:15.790 --> 00:36:18.650
the population in
their own vernacular,

00:36:18.650 --> 00:36:20.540
a traditional language,
and the linguist who

00:36:20.540 --> 00:36:23.050
is, as myself, a
language lover and who

00:36:23.050 --> 00:36:26.050
mourns the death of a
language as a catastrophe,

00:36:26.050 --> 00:36:27.730
those two kinds
of man who do not

00:36:27.730 --> 00:36:31.240
coincide, as you can imagine,
except that some missionaries

00:36:31.240 --> 00:36:34.170
have had linguistic
training of late

00:36:34.170 --> 00:36:37.635
because formerly they did not.

00:36:37.635 --> 00:36:39.510
They translated without
knowing the language.

00:36:39.510 --> 00:36:42.330
I don't know how they
manage, but anyway.

00:36:42.330 --> 00:36:48.520
So these two kind of persons
exert a certain pressure

00:36:48.520 --> 00:36:51.250
on the populations
who are tempted

00:36:51.250 --> 00:36:53.140
to abandon the languages.

00:36:53.140 --> 00:36:57.280
And as a result, they
try to find arguments.

00:36:57.280 --> 00:36:59.530
Here is the plan
of action, which

00:36:59.530 --> 00:37:02.350
is proposed to help
protect against the loss

00:37:02.350 --> 00:37:05.560
of indigenous languages in many
Indian Reservations in America

00:37:05.560 --> 00:37:07.870
and also in Australia.

00:37:07.870 --> 00:37:12.850
Promoting ethnic self-awareness,
confronting apathy

00:37:12.850 --> 00:37:16.330
among young people,
identifying social contacts

00:37:16.330 --> 00:37:20.800
in which traditional
language use can be fostered,

00:37:20.800 --> 00:37:24.490
fighting against the
perception that multilingualism

00:37:24.490 --> 00:37:27.280
is cumbersome and
without great benefits.

00:37:27.280 --> 00:37:30.400
This is what I
myself very often do.

00:37:30.400 --> 00:37:32.620
As a result, there
are some cases

00:37:32.620 --> 00:37:37.850
in which languages escape or at
least provisory escape death.

00:37:37.850 --> 00:37:40.130
Let me mention some examples.

00:37:40.130 --> 00:37:52.720
One of them concerns Indian
reserves in the United States.

00:37:52.720 --> 00:37:55.300
At the beginning of the 16th
century in the lands that

00:37:55.300 --> 00:38:00.070
are now the United States, I
mean the 48 contiguous states

00:38:00.070 --> 00:38:02.320
plus Alaska and
Hawaii, there must

00:38:02.320 --> 00:38:05.770
have been many hundreds
of distinct languages.

00:38:05.770 --> 00:38:07.000
Did you know that?

00:38:07.000 --> 00:38:08.450
Imagine that.

00:38:08.450 --> 00:38:10.510
This is not what the
teachers tell you.

00:38:10.510 --> 00:38:14.560
Well, fewer than
200 still remain.

00:38:14.560 --> 00:38:17.800
And the future of these
is decidedly insecure,

00:38:17.800 --> 00:38:20.840
even where the remoteness
of the location--

00:38:20.840 --> 00:38:24.610
in the case of the Inuit,
Eskimos, of northern Alaska--

00:38:24.610 --> 00:38:29.870
or the large size of the speech
community in the case of whom--

00:38:29.870 --> 00:38:34.220
Navajos of Arizona--
should normally

00:38:34.220 --> 00:38:39.230
or seem to protect the
community from language loss.

00:38:39.230 --> 00:38:43.760
So people launch
programs of revival

00:38:43.760 --> 00:38:46.730
in order to help these
languages not die

00:38:46.730 --> 00:38:50.480
and to make populations
aware of the danger which

00:38:50.480 --> 00:38:55.430
threatens these languages and
threatens them to extinction.

00:38:55.430 --> 00:38:58.610
And this has the result
that some of these languages

00:38:58.610 --> 00:39:03.420
have been revived, although
to some extent artificially,

00:39:03.420 --> 00:39:06.330
but not without success.

00:39:06.330 --> 00:39:08.490
I can mention the
case of Mohawk.

00:39:08.490 --> 00:39:10.410
You have heard of Mohawk?

00:39:10.410 --> 00:39:15.960
Which is still spoken as well
as other Iroquoian family

00:39:15.960 --> 00:39:18.750
because it belongs to the
Iroquoian family, which

00:39:18.750 --> 00:39:22.110
was formerly spoken around the
Great Lakes and the Chicago

00:39:22.110 --> 00:39:24.420
and Detroit region.

00:39:24.420 --> 00:39:27.480
Thanks for the Mohawk
language immersion program,

00:39:27.480 --> 00:39:30.150
the children learned many
of the positive facets

00:39:30.150 --> 00:39:32.520
of Mohawk culture, beyond
the sounds and phrases

00:39:32.520 --> 00:39:33.930
of the Mohawk language.

00:39:33.930 --> 00:39:37.500
Emphasis is also placed on
traditional spirituality

00:39:37.500 --> 00:39:39.930
and respect for teachers
and elders, which

00:39:39.930 --> 00:39:41.340
makes part of their culture.

00:39:41.340 --> 00:39:45.120
The children can identify
with Mohawk role models,

00:39:45.120 --> 00:39:48.570
along with completing all
that is required academically.

00:39:48.570 --> 00:39:50.820
The children have
a feeling of pride

00:39:50.820 --> 00:39:52.750
of knowing their own language.

00:39:52.750 --> 00:39:55.200
The feeling of security
of knowing their heritage

00:39:55.200 --> 00:39:56.250
and culture.

00:39:56.250 --> 00:39:59.610
And the confidence
of a strong identity.

00:39:59.610 --> 00:40:04.590
It has taken 25 years to
arrive at where people are now.

00:40:04.590 --> 00:40:10.160
It has been a struggle, but
tremendously satisfying.

00:40:10.160 --> 00:40:12.320
And my informants
say we still have

00:40:12.320 --> 00:40:15.320
to contend with getting the
community more involved.

00:40:15.320 --> 00:40:19.400
So we are far from being able
to sit back on our laurels.

00:40:19.400 --> 00:40:24.410
So this is a kind of
fighting, almost a warlike,

00:40:24.410 --> 00:40:27.600
campaign against death.

00:40:27.600 --> 00:40:32.570
Now some of them have
succeeded in making

00:40:32.570 --> 00:40:34.940
the political
authorities conscious.

00:40:34.940 --> 00:40:37.070
Before I mention this
fact, let me also

00:40:37.070 --> 00:40:41.090
mention another case
of successful fighting

00:40:41.090 --> 00:40:42.710
against language death.

00:40:42.710 --> 00:40:45.140
This applies to Peach
Spring in Arizona.

00:40:45.140 --> 00:40:47.370
You may have heard of that.

00:40:47.370 --> 00:40:49.400
This is the place
where the Hualapai

00:40:49.400 --> 00:40:51.230
language used to be spoken.

00:40:51.230 --> 00:40:54.020
And at the beginning
of the 20th century,

00:40:54.020 --> 00:40:57.410
it was very, very
severely threatened.

00:40:57.410 --> 00:41:01.730
So people reacted and now
almost all of the teaching staff

00:41:01.730 --> 00:41:03.180
have of the Peach
Springs program

00:41:03.180 --> 00:41:05.430
have attended the American
Indian language Development

00:41:05.430 --> 00:41:10.220
Institute, which was co-founded
by the program's director.

00:41:10.220 --> 00:41:13.700
And as a result of access to
highly trained consultants

00:41:13.700 --> 00:41:16.190
and researchers, this
Peach Springs bilingual,

00:41:16.190 --> 00:41:20.210
bicultural program has produced
a great many highly accurate,

00:41:20.210 --> 00:41:23.960
culturally relevant, and
attractive books and materials

00:41:23.960 --> 00:41:25.670
in the Hualapai
language, one of the most

00:41:25.670 --> 00:41:29.150
threatened among these languages
which belong to the [INAUDIBLE]

00:41:29.150 --> 00:41:29.990
family.

00:41:29.990 --> 00:41:32.180
The program's
publications include books

00:41:32.180 --> 00:41:36.320
on Hualapai ethnobotany,
cattle ranching, hunting,

00:41:36.320 --> 00:41:39.740
traditional foods, and
traditional Hualapai history

00:41:39.740 --> 00:41:41.030
and stories.

00:41:41.030 --> 00:41:42.590
The children in the
school have also

00:41:42.590 --> 00:41:45.720
been involved in creative
writing programs,

00:41:45.720 --> 00:41:49.390
creating contemporary short
stories, life experience

00:41:49.390 --> 00:41:53.890
writing and poetry in their
vernacular Hualapai language.

00:41:53.890 --> 00:41:55.900
Now people have
gone even farther.

00:41:59.170 --> 00:42:04.600
In 1979, an American Indian
Language Development Institute,

00:42:04.600 --> 00:42:07.780
AILDI, was founded.

00:42:07.780 --> 00:42:12.940
And in June 1988, the
International Conference

00:42:12.940 --> 00:42:13.735
of this--

00:42:16.360 --> 00:42:21.790
off the Native American
Language Issues Institute

00:42:21.790 --> 00:42:27.370
held a conference
in Tempe, Arizona.

00:42:27.370 --> 00:42:30.270
This conference, in the
course of the conference,

00:42:30.270 --> 00:42:32.720
all Indian and
non-Indian participants,

00:42:32.720 --> 00:42:34.870
including Hawaiian
representatives,

00:42:34.870 --> 00:42:37.030
worked together to
formulate resolutions

00:42:37.030 --> 00:42:39.610
concerning Native American
languages and cultures.

00:42:39.610 --> 00:42:42.370
In September, a copy
of these resolutions

00:42:42.370 --> 00:42:46.990
was sent to the select
committee of Indian Affairs.

00:42:46.990 --> 00:42:55.360
And later, in 1990,
October, a bill

00:42:55.360 --> 00:42:57.970
was passed by the
Senate and the House.

00:42:57.970 --> 00:43:01.930
And it was signed by President
Bush, the other President Bush.

00:43:01.930 --> 00:43:06.700
The legislation is known
as public law 101 or Native

00:43:06.700 --> 00:43:09.096
American Language Act.

00:43:09.096 --> 00:43:09.970
What does it contain?

00:43:09.970 --> 00:43:12.490
Let me just mention
some articles.

00:43:12.490 --> 00:43:15.310
It says, it is the policy
of the United States

00:43:15.310 --> 00:43:17.950
to first preserve,
protect, and promote

00:43:17.950 --> 00:43:20.470
the rights and freedom
of Native Americans

00:43:20.470 --> 00:43:25.060
to use, practice, and develop
Native American languages.

00:43:25.060 --> 00:43:27.629
Second, encourage
and support the use

00:43:27.629 --> 00:43:29.920
of Native American languages
as a medium of instruction

00:43:29.920 --> 00:43:31.750
in order to
encourage and support

00:43:31.750 --> 00:43:33.340
Native American
language survival,

00:43:33.340 --> 00:43:35.770
educational opportunity,
increase students' success,

00:43:35.770 --> 00:43:37.990
performance, and so on.

00:43:37.990 --> 00:43:40.630
The bill, unfortunately,
in the end

00:43:40.630 --> 00:43:44.800
in a last and little
visible chapter,

00:43:44.800 --> 00:43:48.310
also states that
nothing in this title

00:43:48.310 --> 00:43:52.870
shall be construed as precluding
the use of federal funds

00:43:52.870 --> 00:43:56.060
to teach English to
Native Americans.

00:43:56.060 --> 00:43:59.300
This is enough to--

00:43:59.300 --> 00:44:04.010
for me not to have to comment
upon what this implies.

00:44:04.010 --> 00:44:08.630
Now let me, in order
to say my last words,

00:44:08.630 --> 00:44:13.550
hopefully, say the
reason why the situation,

00:44:13.550 --> 00:44:18.320
as a matter of fact, is not so
dramatic as it might appear.

00:44:18.320 --> 00:44:21.890
This is because the
comparison which

00:44:21.890 --> 00:44:26.120
I referred to as, to a
large extent, metaphorical

00:44:26.120 --> 00:44:28.100
and consequently not
quite scientific,

00:44:28.100 --> 00:44:31.130
between languages
and living species

00:44:31.130 --> 00:44:36.290
has its limits to the extent
that, one, as I announced,

00:44:36.290 --> 00:44:38.570
property of living species.

00:44:38.570 --> 00:44:42.530
I mean, the fact that they die
is not shared by languages.

00:44:42.530 --> 00:44:44.840
Languages, in fact, do not die.

00:44:44.840 --> 00:44:46.100
What does that mean?

00:44:46.100 --> 00:44:46.850
Very simple thing.

00:44:46.850 --> 00:44:48.710
In order to understand
what it implies,

00:44:48.710 --> 00:44:52.610
you have to know that this
is a professional language

00:44:52.610 --> 00:44:54.650
distinction, to some extent.

00:44:54.650 --> 00:44:57.500
It's technical, but
I think everybody--

00:44:57.500 --> 00:45:00.690
are some of you linguists,
major in linguistics?

00:45:00.690 --> 00:45:01.560
None of you?

00:45:01.560 --> 00:45:02.220
Some of you?

00:45:02.220 --> 00:45:08.700
So you must know
that we distinguish

00:45:08.700 --> 00:45:12.660
since the great Swiss-French
linguist Ferdinand de

00:45:12.660 --> 00:45:14.610
Saussure, of whom
you may have heard,

00:45:14.610 --> 00:45:18.120
we distinguish between
parole and langue, language

00:45:18.120 --> 00:45:23.400
as a system and speech as
the use of this system.

00:45:23.400 --> 00:45:26.910
Lang, or language,
as an abstract notion

00:45:26.910 --> 00:45:30.300
means any instrument
of communication which

00:45:30.300 --> 00:45:33.030
possesses a phonetic system--

00:45:33.030 --> 00:45:38.750
because you need sounds in order
to make, to fabricate words,

00:45:38.750 --> 00:45:43.470
a morphology, which is a study
of the external form of words,

00:45:43.470 --> 00:45:46.380
because you need words
made of sounds in order

00:45:46.380 --> 00:45:49.980
to be the vectors of
semantic contents,

00:45:49.980 --> 00:45:54.360
then syntax because you
need an environment in order

00:45:54.360 --> 00:45:59.100
to build sentences in the
framework of which these words

00:45:59.100 --> 00:46:00.540
will be put.

00:46:00.540 --> 00:46:03.570
So it is the job of
syntax to study this.

00:46:03.570 --> 00:46:05.670
And finally, you
need semantics, which

00:46:05.670 --> 00:46:09.690
is the study of the content
of these words, which

00:46:09.690 --> 00:46:13.050
are made of sounds which are--

00:46:13.050 --> 00:46:16.200
which these sounds constituting
together the morphemes,

00:46:16.200 --> 00:46:19.200
and these morphemes together
according to a certain word

00:46:19.200 --> 00:46:21.220
order, constituting sentences.

00:46:21.220 --> 00:46:23.190
So this is the
system of language.

00:46:23.190 --> 00:46:26.760
You can study this in any
language of the world.

00:46:26.760 --> 00:46:32.790
And another parameter,
another direction of language

00:46:32.790 --> 00:46:36.090
is speech, what the
French call parole.

00:46:36.090 --> 00:46:37.170
What does it mean?

00:46:37.170 --> 00:46:40.410
It means the way you
put all that into use,

00:46:40.410 --> 00:46:48.270
the way you utilize these
performance possibilities

00:46:48.270 --> 00:46:50.200
in order to communicate.

00:46:50.200 --> 00:46:54.270
And this is the only things
which, in fact, dies.

00:46:54.270 --> 00:46:57.540
But language is a system,
phonetic, morphologic,

00:46:57.540 --> 00:46:59.850
syntactic, and
semantic, does not die.

00:46:59.850 --> 00:47:01.110
Why does it not die?

00:47:01.110 --> 00:47:04.320
For a simple reason,
why should it die?

00:47:04.320 --> 00:47:07.240
When we say that a
language is dead,

00:47:07.240 --> 00:47:09.450
we mean that parole is dead.

00:47:09.450 --> 00:47:10.920
It means that it
is out of usage.

00:47:10.920 --> 00:47:13.000
People do not use it any longer.

00:47:13.000 --> 00:47:18.540
But provided we have kept a
description of this language,

00:47:18.540 --> 00:47:20.890
of course, in the opposite
case it is completely dead

00:47:20.890 --> 00:47:23.610
and without any
hope, hopelessly.

00:47:23.610 --> 00:47:28.290
However, when we have
kept descriptions written,

00:47:28.290 --> 00:47:31.530
as is often the case, either by
missionaries or by grammarians,

00:47:31.530 --> 00:47:33.240
what used to be
grammarians formerly,

00:47:33.240 --> 00:47:34.980
and what is to be
today linguists,

00:47:34.980 --> 00:47:37.200
professional
linguists like myself.

00:47:37.200 --> 00:47:41.100
When we have descriptions, then
provided we can explore them.

00:47:41.100 --> 00:47:44.310
Provided we can tap
them and see what

00:47:44.310 --> 00:47:48.240
they describe the language,
which is called dead,

00:47:48.240 --> 00:47:50.400
which is assumed to
have disappeared.

00:47:50.400 --> 00:47:54.300
We can revive this
language because we know

00:47:54.300 --> 00:47:56.160
on what basis to revive this.

00:47:56.160 --> 00:48:00.600
And this is what has been done
in at least one very special

00:48:00.600 --> 00:48:07.410
case which I cannot not
mention to end my talk.

00:48:07.410 --> 00:48:10.110
And this is the case of Hebrew.

00:48:10.110 --> 00:48:12.870
I cannot avoid mentioning
it because it is,

00:48:12.870 --> 00:48:18.420
although reconstituted revived
in a very special and probably

00:48:18.420 --> 00:48:21.510
difficult to reproduce, in
very special circumstances,

00:48:21.510 --> 00:48:23.730
it has been revived,
as a matter of fact.

00:48:23.730 --> 00:48:27.060
And this, of course, cannot
not be cited as a model.

00:48:27.060 --> 00:48:29.370
What does this consist of?

00:48:29.370 --> 00:48:35.070
You probably know that
when Jesus of Nazareth,

00:48:35.070 --> 00:48:37.200
the Jewish way of calling him.

00:48:37.200 --> 00:48:40.820
He was Yeshua and Nazareth was
the city in which he lived.

00:48:40.820 --> 00:48:42.970
He was born in
Bethlehem, but Bethlehem

00:48:42.970 --> 00:48:45.690
which means the house of bread.

00:48:45.690 --> 00:48:48.180
Beth is house and
Lehem is the bread.

00:48:48.180 --> 00:48:49.860
He was born in the
house of bread.

00:48:49.860 --> 00:48:51.930
But he spent most of
his life in Nazareth.

00:48:51.930 --> 00:48:54.039
And the Jewish way
of calling him--

00:48:54.039 --> 00:48:55.830
of course we do not
recognize Jesus Christ.

00:48:55.830 --> 00:48:57.630
Christ means anointed.

00:48:57.630 --> 00:49:00.210
This is Christ Catholic, it
does not belong to our culture.

00:49:00.210 --> 00:49:03.400
So I call it the Jewish
way, Jesus of Nazareth,

00:49:03.400 --> 00:49:05.520
which was his name for
his contemporaries.

00:49:05.520 --> 00:49:09.480
When he appeared, Hebrew
was already a dead language

00:49:09.480 --> 00:49:14.340
and had been such for
500 years, 600 years.

00:49:14.340 --> 00:49:15.090
Why?

00:49:15.090 --> 00:49:22.130
Because in minus 594,
the Jews were exiled in--

00:49:22.130 --> 00:49:24.780
you know where-- in Babylon.

00:49:24.780 --> 00:49:25.350
Why?

00:49:25.350 --> 00:49:28.310
Because Nebuchadnezzar,
whose name

00:49:28.310 --> 00:49:32.450
[INAUDIBLE] in a very well-known
Verdi opera, Nebuchadnezzar.

00:49:32.450 --> 00:49:35.310
Nebuchadnezzar in
a Syrian language,

00:49:35.310 --> 00:49:42.220
had fought against the Judea and
defeated the Jews and the Jews

00:49:42.220 --> 00:49:49.680
[? Eretz, ?] the aristocracy,
the king himself,

00:49:49.680 --> 00:49:56.400
and all the ones who ruled,
and high [INAUDIBLE]..

00:49:56.400 --> 00:49:58.170
All these people
were exiled and they

00:49:58.170 --> 00:50:00.210
were forced to go to Babylon.

00:50:00.210 --> 00:50:03.870
And of course, then,
six centuries before

00:50:03.870 --> 00:50:07.110
the birth of Yeshua of
Nazareth, they spoke Hebrew.

00:50:07.110 --> 00:50:09.330
But then, being
exalted in Babylon,

00:50:09.330 --> 00:50:11.640
they progressively
adopted the language,

00:50:11.640 --> 00:50:14.040
which was very
widespread then in what

00:50:14.040 --> 00:50:15.690
we call Middle East today.

00:50:15.690 --> 00:50:17.400
And this language,
you know what it was?

00:50:17.400 --> 00:50:23.280
It was Aramean, not Armenian,
Aramean, which is not yet dead,

00:50:23.280 --> 00:50:26.090
probably the oldest still
remaining, still living

00:50:26.090 --> 00:50:28.470
language in the world,
which is still spoken today,

00:50:28.470 --> 00:50:30.480
of course in a new
form, in modern form,

00:50:30.480 --> 00:50:33.720
in the heights of some
Lebanese mountains.

00:50:33.720 --> 00:50:36.540
And this Aramean
language became--

00:50:36.540 --> 00:50:38.670
although originally it did
not belong to the Jews,

00:50:38.670 --> 00:50:41.100
it became a very Jewish
language, in a way,

00:50:41.100 --> 00:50:43.200
because it was adopted
by Jewish Eretz.

00:50:43.200 --> 00:50:47.320
After the exile, when they
came back to Jerusalem,

00:50:47.320 --> 00:50:49.380
they did not speak
Hebrew anymore.

00:50:49.380 --> 00:50:51.210
What did they speak, Aramean.

00:50:51.210 --> 00:50:54.540
So Jesus of Nazareth
spoke Aramean

00:50:54.540 --> 00:50:56.970
like all his contemporaries.

00:50:56.970 --> 00:50:59.480
And he very probably--

00:50:59.480 --> 00:51:02.850
we are pretty sure-- that the
way, the language in which he

00:51:02.850 --> 00:51:08.790
answered the accusations
when he was sued was Aramean.

00:51:08.790 --> 00:51:12.600
Of course, he-- the rabbis
asked him whether he

00:51:12.600 --> 00:51:14.730
knew the prayers, he did.

00:51:14.730 --> 00:51:18.000
And he probably said
the prayers in Hebrew

00:51:18.000 --> 00:51:21.620
because Hebrew was and still
is, in Jewish communities today,

00:51:21.620 --> 00:51:24.030
the language of
liturgia, the language

00:51:24.030 --> 00:51:26.910
which is used in the
synagogue and which

00:51:26.910 --> 00:51:30.000
is a religious language.

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:34.300
But as soon as the
birth of Christ,

00:51:34.300 --> 00:51:36.600
let us call him
the Christian way,

00:51:36.600 --> 00:51:40.350
as soon as the birth of Christ,
Hebrew was already dead.

00:51:40.350 --> 00:51:44.680
And it was so since
six centuries before.

00:51:44.680 --> 00:51:50.100
So in 1948, the
state of Israel was

00:51:50.100 --> 00:51:54.540
created by a vote in the United
Nations, which unfortunately,

00:51:54.540 --> 00:51:58.110
as soon as it was
published, brought

00:51:58.110 --> 00:52:01.350
about the dramatic departure
of all the representatives

00:52:01.350 --> 00:52:02.520
of Arabic countries.

00:52:02.520 --> 00:52:07.980
They all stood up and they left
the hall in which this election

00:52:07.980 --> 00:52:13.020
had taken place as a kind
of symbolic rejection

00:52:13.020 --> 00:52:16.030
at the very beginning of
the creation of Israel.

00:52:16.030 --> 00:52:21.480
This of course, you know
the results still today

00:52:21.480 --> 00:52:23.680
under our eyes of
this situation.

00:52:23.680 --> 00:52:25.740
However, when the state--

00:52:25.740 --> 00:52:28.920
in such a difficult context--

00:52:28.920 --> 00:52:31.530
was created by an
international resolution,

00:52:31.530 --> 00:52:37.020
then the Hebrew language had
already began to be revived.

00:52:37.020 --> 00:52:37.890
How?

00:52:37.890 --> 00:52:39.960
Because there was a man--

00:52:39.960 --> 00:52:41.940
of course, it's not the
work of a single man.

00:52:41.940 --> 00:52:44.430
You have heard
probably of Ben Yehuda.

00:52:44.430 --> 00:52:48.120
Ben Yehuda was a
Jewish Russian who,

00:52:48.120 --> 00:52:52.080
at the end of the 19th
century, all of a sudden

00:52:52.080 --> 00:52:55.077
became aware of the
fact that it was not

00:52:55.077 --> 00:52:56.910
enough-- most of these
people were Zionists,

00:52:56.910 --> 00:53:00.960
not in the very derogative
sense of today, unfortunately.

00:53:00.960 --> 00:53:04.740
Zionist then meant an
ideal of liberation.

00:53:04.740 --> 00:53:08.130
To be Zionist at the end of
19th century, as Ben Yehuda was,

00:53:08.130 --> 00:53:11.910
implied to be
inhabited by a desire

00:53:11.910 --> 00:53:22.590
to free the Jewish people from
slavery and from anti-Semitism

00:53:22.590 --> 00:53:27.880
and all its negative
aspect of its history.

00:53:27.880 --> 00:53:32.940
So they were Zionists in this
meaning, to which in order

00:53:32.940 --> 00:53:35.920
to free themselves
from their binds.

00:53:35.920 --> 00:53:40.770
And then he, all of a
sudden, became aware

00:53:40.770 --> 00:53:47.670
that in order to be
able to go in Palestine,

00:53:47.670 --> 00:53:50.610
the Jews had to speak
a language which was--

00:53:50.610 --> 00:53:52.770
which should be
common to all of them.

00:53:52.770 --> 00:53:54.020
And then many Jews--

00:53:54.020 --> 00:53:57.130
this was far, very long
before the creation of Israel,

00:53:57.130 --> 00:53:59.580
it was in the beginning
of the 20th century.

00:53:59.580 --> 00:54:01.620
And of the 19th and
beginning of the 20th.

00:54:01.620 --> 00:54:05.040
Many Jews made what we
call in Hebrew aliyah.

00:54:05.040 --> 00:54:06.900
They went to Palestine.

00:54:06.900 --> 00:54:10.110
Many of them came from
Russia because as you know,

00:54:10.110 --> 00:54:12.600
Czarist Russia had
many, many programs

00:54:12.600 --> 00:54:16.770
and the Jews were obliged
to flee from Russia in order

00:54:16.770 --> 00:54:18.900
to avoid murder or slaughter.

00:54:18.900 --> 00:54:22.470
And then they went to
Palestine, which was then

00:54:22.470 --> 00:54:28.680
a Turkish province administered
and ruled by Constantinople,

00:54:28.680 --> 00:54:30.330
by the Turks.

00:54:30.330 --> 00:54:34.740
Then in 1948, when the
state of Israel was created,

00:54:34.740 --> 00:54:39.870
Hebrew was already a spoken
language thanks to the efforts

00:54:39.870 --> 00:54:47.260
of Ben Yehuda and other ones who
spent almost their whole life

00:54:47.260 --> 00:54:51.389
looking for words of the Bible,
modernizing the language.

00:54:51.389 --> 00:54:52.930
Of course, the Bible
does not contain

00:54:52.930 --> 00:54:58.720
words to say atomic bomb or
airplane or terrorism and so

00:54:58.720 --> 00:55:01.960
on, notions which belong to
our context, cultural context.

00:55:01.960 --> 00:55:04.120
But they made up these words.

00:55:04.120 --> 00:55:05.560
They invented.

00:55:05.560 --> 00:55:06.910
They coined new words.

00:55:06.910 --> 00:55:10.840
And this neurological work,
which was extremely important,

00:55:10.840 --> 00:55:12.820
took years and
years, during which

00:55:12.820 --> 00:55:18.490
a new language, not quite new,
but a modernization of Hebrew

00:55:18.490 --> 00:55:21.010
finally was possible.

00:55:21.010 --> 00:55:24.760
And this-- which is
called Israeli Hebrew--

00:55:24.760 --> 00:55:26.590
was in fact a revival of Hebrew.

00:55:26.590 --> 00:55:27.190
Why?

00:55:27.190 --> 00:55:29.080
Because the syntax,
the morphology,

00:55:29.080 --> 00:55:32.650
of course, the vocabulary
as I said, is renewed.

00:55:32.650 --> 00:55:36.460
But the core of the language,
syntax morphology, and also

00:55:36.460 --> 00:55:40.740
phonology, to the extent that
we knew how they pronounced

00:55:40.740 --> 00:55:43.510
three millennia
ago, were the same

00:55:43.510 --> 00:55:45.910
as those of a biblical Hebrew.

00:55:45.910 --> 00:55:49.520
So BI, Biblical Hebrew, and IH--

00:55:49.520 --> 00:55:52.702
BH, Biblical Hebrew,
and IH, Israeli Hebrew,

00:55:52.702 --> 00:55:54.160
are more or less
the same language.

00:55:54.160 --> 00:55:57.280
Of course with differences
which can be explained

00:55:57.280 --> 00:56:01.870
easily when you think
of the period of time

00:56:01.870 --> 00:56:03.370
which separates them.

00:56:03.370 --> 00:56:05.380
So this language was revived.

00:56:05.380 --> 00:56:08.650
When I say revived, I mean
that not only was Hebrew

00:56:08.650 --> 00:56:11.500
a dead language as soon as the
beginning of the Christian era,

00:56:11.500 --> 00:56:13.960
as I said, due to
the Babylon exile,

00:56:13.960 --> 00:56:18.310
but also Hebrew
had never been used

00:56:18.310 --> 00:56:21.880
except in quite an
exclusively religious context.

00:56:21.880 --> 00:56:24.010
It was the language
of Talmudists,

00:56:24.010 --> 00:56:27.060
of rabbis, and their
scientific correspondence,

00:56:27.060 --> 00:56:29.710
of learned people, but not
the language in which we

00:56:29.710 --> 00:56:32.470
say to a girl, I love
you, or to your father,

00:56:32.470 --> 00:56:37.150
please give me a fork or a
spoon when you are eating.

00:56:37.150 --> 00:56:39.610
So it was the language
which was used exclusively

00:56:39.610 --> 00:56:44.560
in very solemn and very
religious circumstances, never

00:56:44.560 --> 00:56:46.040
in everyday life.

00:56:46.040 --> 00:56:47.950
This is the reason why
I said it was dead,

00:56:47.950 --> 00:56:49.930
because the language
which was used extensively

00:56:49.930 --> 00:56:52.660
between learned people
and in written form

00:56:52.660 --> 00:56:54.530
but not in other form
is a dead language.

00:56:54.530 --> 00:56:59.200
However, I mentioned the
example to terminate this talk

00:56:59.200 --> 00:57:04.090
as a proof that when the
language has been described,

00:57:04.090 --> 00:57:06.580
when we have many, many
texts, the main of them being

00:57:06.580 --> 00:57:10.660
the Bible itself, then in
spite of its having appeared,

00:57:10.660 --> 00:57:13.180
in spite of its
having never been used

00:57:13.180 --> 00:57:14.830
during [INAUDIBLE]
and [INAUDIBLE],,

00:57:14.830 --> 00:57:16.990
it is not quite dead
because it can be revived

00:57:16.990 --> 00:57:19.670
since we have the testimonies.

00:57:19.670 --> 00:57:21.280
We have the elements.

00:57:21.280 --> 00:57:23.720
Of course, this is one
thing which is requested.

00:57:23.720 --> 00:57:27.940
Another one is a certain
will, a collective will,

00:57:27.940 --> 00:57:32.590
a very strong will
shared by many people

00:57:32.590 --> 00:57:36.100
to revive a language,
of which we have

00:57:36.100 --> 00:57:37.630
at our disposal the materials.

00:57:37.630 --> 00:57:40.930
This will, as far as I know,
to the best of my knowledge,

00:57:40.930 --> 00:57:47.030
has never been illustrated,
excluding among the Jews.

00:57:47.030 --> 00:57:49.990
But I think this is a
model to be exploited,

00:57:49.990 --> 00:57:55.380
to be utilized as things to be
imitated by other communities.

00:57:55.380 --> 00:57:59.310
The inhabitants of
Cornwall in England,

00:57:59.310 --> 00:58:02.980
in the south of England,
have tried along this model

00:58:02.980 --> 00:58:04.420
to revive Cornish.

00:58:04.420 --> 00:58:07.330
Cornish was dead at the
end of the 17th century.

00:58:07.330 --> 00:58:12.310
And the Corn-- how
to say Cornwalls?

00:58:12.310 --> 00:58:13.190
AUDIENCE: Cornish.

00:58:13.190 --> 00:58:16.520
HAGEGE: Cornish, who of
course speak English and have

00:58:16.520 --> 00:58:19.310
done so for centuries
and centuries said,

00:58:19.310 --> 00:58:20.090
we are not Jews.

00:58:20.090 --> 00:58:20.965
In fact, they're not.

00:58:20.965 --> 00:58:21.470
It's true.

00:58:21.470 --> 00:58:29.030
But why wouldn't we try to do
the same thing as the Jews did.

00:58:29.030 --> 00:58:34.210
And so they have succeeded
against all expectations,

00:58:34.210 --> 00:58:37.380
a very, very queer,
an odd success.

00:58:37.380 --> 00:58:42.990
They have succeeded
at dazzling speed

00:58:42.990 --> 00:58:49.170
to reviving their language
along the model of Hebrew.

00:58:49.170 --> 00:58:52.960
We have some other
cases which are Creoles,

00:58:52.960 --> 00:58:55.600
such as the one used
in the east of Timor

00:58:55.600 --> 00:58:58.900
and also the one
Nagamese used in Nagaland

00:58:58.900 --> 00:59:03.820
which is a Himalayan states at
the extreme Northeast of India.

00:59:03.820 --> 00:59:05.120
We have some other examples.

00:59:05.120 --> 00:59:11.320
But the only one we can
mention and which is a sure one

00:59:11.320 --> 00:59:13.450
is Hebrew, revived Hebrew.

00:59:13.450 --> 00:59:16.270
And this illustrates,
once more, as I said,

00:59:16.270 --> 00:59:19.300
the fact that when
parole I mean,

00:59:19.300 --> 00:59:22.289
the use in everyday speech
is the only parameter which

00:59:22.289 --> 00:59:22.830
has appeared.

00:59:22.830 --> 00:59:26.680
The language itself has not
because it is described, then,

00:59:26.680 --> 00:59:30.490
if I dare say so, it's suffices
to have a collective will

00:59:30.490 --> 00:59:31.930
to revive the language.

00:59:31.930 --> 00:59:35.090
Of course, this collective
will does not always exist.

00:59:35.090 --> 00:59:37.780
And the destiny, the
fate of the Jewish people

00:59:37.780 --> 00:59:41.230
is not a fate which is very
common, as you may know.

00:59:41.230 --> 00:59:45.180
So this is the
last thing I wanted

00:59:45.180 --> 00:59:48.820
to mention in order to show--

00:59:48.820 --> 00:59:51.010
no, my last, last
thing is that--

00:59:54.460 --> 01:00:02.680
other than Hebrew-- we can
mention facts which relieve us

01:00:02.680 --> 01:00:06.370
from this despair,
from the dispiriting--

01:00:06.370 --> 01:00:10.160
from this situation in which
we see so many languages dying.

01:00:10.160 --> 01:00:15.069
We have also some languages
which appear from nothing,

01:00:15.069 --> 01:00:15.610
from scratch.

01:00:15.610 --> 01:00:18.269
And these languages are Creoles.

01:00:18.269 --> 01:00:19.060
This is well known.

01:00:19.060 --> 01:00:20.110
I am, myself, a Creolist.

01:00:20.110 --> 01:00:23.410
Creolistics, I would
like to recall,

01:00:23.410 --> 01:00:27.820
Creolistics is one of the
most vivid and most active

01:00:27.820 --> 01:00:29.680
disciplines within linguistics.

01:00:29.680 --> 01:00:30.730
I am a Creolist myself.

01:00:30.730 --> 01:00:31.230
Why?

01:00:31.230 --> 01:00:35.620
Because we see in West
Indies, in New Hebrides,

01:00:35.620 --> 01:00:37.822
in other parts of
the world, we know

01:00:37.822 --> 01:00:39.280
languages that are
extremely young,

01:00:39.280 --> 01:00:41.320
we can assign them two
or three centuries.

01:00:41.320 --> 01:00:43.840
And we know when they were
born, when they appeared.

01:00:43.840 --> 01:00:45.490
And these Creoles
are languages which

01:00:45.490 --> 01:00:48.820
have been created of late,
two or three centuries ago,

01:00:48.820 --> 01:00:53.110
for the oldest of them, by
populations which were deprived

01:00:53.110 --> 01:00:55.300
of other languages
due to slavery

01:00:55.300 --> 01:00:59.200
and to a very sad history,
which you may know.

01:00:59.200 --> 01:01:03.310
So the birth of
Creoles, of course,

01:01:03.310 --> 01:01:08.000
does not compensate for the
death of some of the languages.

01:01:08.000 --> 01:01:11.680
But it is a fact to be
mentioned as a sign of hope

01:01:11.680 --> 01:01:15.940
that when many language die,
some languages also appear.

01:01:15.940 --> 01:01:21.280
And this process might go on
during centuries and centuries,

01:01:21.280 --> 01:01:22.340
I believe.

01:01:22.340 --> 01:01:24.090
Thank you very much,
for your attention.

01:01:24.090 --> 01:01:27.060
[APPLAUSE]

01:02:01.710 --> 01:02:03.195
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

01:02:12.140 --> 01:02:14.450
HAGEGE: Yes, but
this is precisely why

01:02:14.450 --> 01:02:17.990
I say that the metaphorical
comparison of languages

01:02:17.990 --> 01:02:20.510
with living species
finds its limitations

01:02:20.510 --> 01:02:22.280
in this very argument.

01:02:22.280 --> 01:02:25.152
Species, when they are dead,
are dead, definitely, which

01:02:25.152 --> 01:02:26.360
is not the case of languages.

01:02:26.360 --> 01:02:28.810
This is exactly what I said.

01:02:28.810 --> 01:02:30.390
You cannot revive a--

01:02:30.390 --> 01:02:31.960
if you are an
entomologist, you know

01:02:31.960 --> 01:02:33.740
that a special
species of insects

01:02:33.740 --> 01:02:37.000
has disappeared from the earth
for economical, ecological

01:02:37.000 --> 01:02:39.310
reasons that you can
study, then no one

01:02:39.310 --> 01:02:42.280
will be able to revive it, which
is not the case of languages,

01:02:42.280 --> 01:02:47.650
provided you have situations
like the ones I referred to.

01:02:47.650 --> 01:02:48.840
And your other question?

01:02:48.840 --> 01:02:50.214
AUDIENCE: About
Creole languages.

01:02:50.214 --> 01:02:52.540
You think of them
as being young--

01:02:52.540 --> 01:02:53.530
HAGEGE: Yes, they are.

01:02:53.530 --> 01:02:54.613
AUDIENCE: Local creations.

01:02:54.613 --> 01:02:55.190
[INAUDIBLE]

01:02:57.720 --> 01:02:58.920
By which measure?

01:02:58.920 --> 01:02:59.670
What measure--

01:02:59.670 --> 01:03:02.520
HAGEGE: Because you know
where they were born.

01:03:02.520 --> 01:03:03.819
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

01:03:03.819 --> 01:03:05.360
HAGEGE: Would you
like some examples?

01:03:05.360 --> 01:03:05.985
AUDIENCE: Sure.

01:03:05.985 --> 01:03:06.559
[INAUDIBLE]

01:03:06.559 --> 01:03:07.850
HAGEGE: You know the situation.

01:03:07.850 --> 01:03:08.580
Well?

01:03:08.580 --> 01:03:11.520
AUDIENCE: Haitian
Creole, as an example.

01:03:11.520 --> 01:03:14.520
[INAUDIBLE]

01:03:14.520 --> 01:03:17.450
HAGEGE: You know,
I have myself--

01:03:17.450 --> 01:03:19.380
well, Haitian, you say?

01:03:19.380 --> 01:03:21.540
Well, I know well--

01:03:21.540 --> 01:03:23.460
I know better, although
it's more of the same

01:03:23.460 --> 01:03:25.459
because they can communicate
without difficulty.

01:03:25.459 --> 01:03:27.000
I know better the
ones which belong

01:03:27.000 --> 01:03:30.790
to French territories
still today,

01:03:30.790 --> 01:03:33.785
which are the one of Guadalupe,
Martinique, and Guyana.

01:03:33.785 --> 01:03:35.160
We know really
well, when I speak

01:03:35.160 --> 01:03:37.700
of Creole appearing
at a certain date,

01:03:37.700 --> 01:03:40.020
it is because we can
date their appearance.

01:03:40.020 --> 01:03:41.640
And why?

01:03:41.640 --> 01:03:46.770
This is related to the
history of slavery, you know.

01:03:46.770 --> 01:03:50.550
African communities were
taken away from their tribes

01:03:50.550 --> 01:03:56.540
where they lived
by merchants, who

01:03:56.540 --> 01:03:59.880
were Spanish, French,
mainly British,

01:03:59.880 --> 01:04:02.110
but also from other
countries, Portuguese.

01:04:02.110 --> 01:04:04.800
They were bought
from local chiefs.

01:04:04.800 --> 01:04:08.400
And then as soon
as they embarked

01:04:08.400 --> 01:04:10.950
in these boat which led
them to the colonies,

01:04:10.950 --> 01:04:13.770
the tribes were mixed
up and the languages

01:04:13.770 --> 01:04:15.830
were being disappearing.

01:04:15.830 --> 01:04:17.970
Then this is a well-known story.

01:04:17.970 --> 01:04:21.050
I apologize for recalling it,
but you know it is well known.

01:04:21.050 --> 01:04:24.720
Then they are obliged to create
new means of communication

01:04:24.720 --> 01:04:25.920
in order to communicate.

01:04:25.920 --> 01:04:28.680
And what they create was
a kind of new language

01:04:28.680 --> 01:04:32.070
in which they took some
things from the language

01:04:32.070 --> 01:04:36.040
of the masters, French, English,
Spanish, as the case may be,

01:04:36.040 --> 01:04:39.210
and some other elements from
their own vernacular languages.

01:04:39.210 --> 01:04:41.371
And this is why
Creolistics has become

01:04:41.371 --> 01:04:42.870
such an important
part of linguistic

01:04:42.870 --> 01:04:44.880
because we have
great controversies.

01:04:44.880 --> 01:04:47.190
We have what we call
the substratists

01:04:47.190 --> 01:04:48.690
and the anti-substratists.

01:04:48.690 --> 01:04:50.130
I am a substratists, I mean.

01:04:50.130 --> 01:04:51.540
I am among the those.

01:04:51.540 --> 01:04:54.330
I am an African, is
myself, so I have studied

01:04:54.330 --> 01:04:56.190
many, many African languages.

01:04:56.190 --> 01:05:00.150
And I recognize, I identify
in Creole as many African

01:05:00.150 --> 01:05:02.730
features, which cannot be
explained by French, English,

01:05:02.730 --> 01:05:03.720
or Spanish.

01:05:03.720 --> 01:05:07.260
And the anti-Creolists,
they retain other features

01:05:07.260 --> 01:05:09.600
which are the same as the
ones in European languages.

01:05:09.600 --> 01:05:11.430
And they say, no.

01:05:11.430 --> 01:05:12.720
You cannot be a substratists.

01:05:12.720 --> 01:05:14.070
We are anti-substratists.

01:05:14.070 --> 01:05:16.680
So the controversy
consists of saying,

01:05:16.680 --> 01:05:18.390
what are the traits,
the features which

01:05:18.390 --> 01:05:20.410
are due to the African
origin of slaves?

01:05:20.410 --> 01:05:23.610
And what are the ones which
are assignable to the language

01:05:23.610 --> 01:05:25.688
of their masters, which
are European languages.

01:05:25.688 --> 01:05:28.104
AUDIENCE: How is that different
form the history of France

01:05:28.104 --> 01:05:28.790
where you have--

01:05:28.790 --> 01:05:30.290
HAGEGE: I didn't
say the difference.

01:05:30.290 --> 01:05:31.653
I just say it is more recent.

01:05:31.653 --> 01:05:35.239
AUDIENCE: So then why would
Creole be a new species?

01:05:35.239 --> 01:05:36.030
HAGEGE: No, no, no.

01:05:36.030 --> 01:05:38.470
I don't say that.

01:05:38.470 --> 01:05:40.570
I understand what you imply.

01:05:40.570 --> 01:05:43.110
I'm not saying that Creoles
are something quite apart

01:05:43.110 --> 01:05:45.660
and not to be compared with
other kinds of language.

01:05:45.660 --> 01:05:47.490
They are human languages
like any other.

01:05:47.490 --> 01:05:49.830
I just say that they are,
by far, younger than the one

01:05:49.830 --> 01:05:50.610
you know.

01:05:50.610 --> 01:05:51.240
Why?

01:05:51.240 --> 01:05:55.920
Because the origin of Latin
and Greek is extremely remote

01:05:55.920 --> 01:05:57.520
and we don't have
enough documents.

01:05:57.520 --> 01:05:59.520
The origin of Creoles
is very recent

01:05:59.520 --> 01:06:05.670
because we know when these
slavery circumstances began.

01:06:05.670 --> 01:06:07.834
We know most of the African
languages still in use,

01:06:07.834 --> 01:06:09.750
of course, which were
the ones that the tribes

01:06:09.750 --> 01:06:11.700
to which these slaves belonged.

01:06:11.700 --> 01:06:13.710
And then the only
thing I say is not

01:06:13.710 --> 01:06:15.660
that they are
different, by any means,

01:06:15.660 --> 01:06:17.370
but they are younger
because we know

01:06:17.370 --> 01:06:19.800
where and when they appeared.

01:06:19.800 --> 01:06:21.840
This is what I say.

01:06:21.840 --> 01:06:25.890
Most Creolists will have the
same language because we--

01:06:25.890 --> 01:06:28.890
the reason among--
yes, just a minute.

01:06:28.890 --> 01:06:31.770
Among the reason we
mentioned for giving

01:06:31.770 --> 01:06:35.550
to Creolistics such an important
place within our studies

01:06:35.550 --> 01:06:38.310
is that not only
this controversy

01:06:38.310 --> 01:06:40.350
between substratists
and anti-substratists

01:06:40.350 --> 01:06:47.190
is in fact a very important
theoretical questioning

01:06:47.190 --> 01:06:52.140
linguistics because it, in
fact, refers to what language is

01:06:52.140 --> 01:06:54.060
and how does a language appear.

01:06:54.060 --> 01:06:58.800
So Creoles provide us arguments
in one or other direction.

01:06:58.800 --> 01:07:02.370
The other reason is, as I said,
that we know when they appeared

01:07:02.370 --> 01:07:05.530
and then what we cannot
do for Latin, Hebrew,

01:07:05.530 --> 01:07:08.160
Arabic and other languages,
of which old documents have

01:07:08.160 --> 01:07:10.440
disappeared, we can do for
Creoles because we have

01:07:10.440 --> 01:07:11.100
documents.

01:07:11.100 --> 01:07:16.020
Missionaries who evangelized the
slaves spoke African languages

01:07:16.020 --> 01:07:17.370
with them, described them.

01:07:17.370 --> 01:07:18.300
And they saw.

01:07:18.300 --> 01:07:23.160
They were a witness of the
birth of these languages.

01:07:23.160 --> 01:07:24.900
They saw them
appearing progressively

01:07:24.900 --> 01:07:27.060
and we have descriptions,
great number.

01:07:27.060 --> 01:07:30.699
This is why Creolistics, to me,
and to many other linguists,

01:07:30.699 --> 01:07:32.490
is such an important
domain in linguistics.

01:07:32.490 --> 01:07:33.908
Yes?

01:07:33.908 --> 01:07:36.812
AUDIENCE: What's your comment
about the multiplication

01:07:36.812 --> 01:07:38.748
of languages of
different [INAUDIBLE]

01:07:38.748 --> 01:07:41.180
that can be seen, for
instance, over the internet.

01:07:41.180 --> 01:07:43.970
Every site, every [INAUDIBLE]
has got its own language

01:07:43.970 --> 01:07:47.210
that nobody else can
understand or use.

01:07:47.210 --> 01:07:50.476
There is no oral
tradition for that,

01:07:50.476 --> 01:07:52.865
but every community
has got it's own way

01:07:52.865 --> 01:07:54.006
of speak and communicate.

01:07:54.006 --> 01:07:55.780
So what's your
comment about that?

01:07:55.780 --> 01:07:56.505
HAGEGE: What?

01:07:56.505 --> 01:07:57.690
AUDIENCE: What's your
opinion about that.

01:07:57.690 --> 01:07:58.700
HAGEGE: The internet?

01:07:58.700 --> 01:08:01.850
AUDIENCE: Yeah, or
different [INAUDIBLE]..

01:08:01.850 --> 01:08:06.070
HAGEGE: I see internet as a
big chance for the diversity

01:08:06.070 --> 01:08:07.490
for multilingualism.

01:08:07.490 --> 01:08:11.750
Because in the beginning
internet appeared wrongly.

01:08:11.750 --> 01:08:17.390
We thought appeared as one
more way of promoting English.

01:08:17.390 --> 01:08:22.370
But progressively,
internet was--

01:08:22.370 --> 01:08:25.399
people became aware
of the possibility

01:08:25.399 --> 01:08:28.130
which was provided by internet
to promote their own languages.

01:08:28.130 --> 01:08:31.580
And now what I see,
myself almost every day,

01:08:31.580 --> 01:08:33.979
is that many, many
sites are occupied

01:08:33.979 --> 01:08:36.020
by languages which we
would not have expected

01:08:36.020 --> 01:08:37.790
to appear in an internet.

01:08:37.790 --> 01:08:41.450
So internet, for me, right
now, is one of the best ways

01:08:41.450 --> 01:08:44.806
to preserve language
from disappearing.

01:08:44.806 --> 01:08:45.790
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

01:08:45.790 --> 01:08:47.306
HAGEGE: It was
not your question?

01:08:47.306 --> 01:08:49.556
AUDIENCE: Yeah, my question
is that many new languages

01:08:49.556 --> 01:08:50.270
are appearing.

01:08:50.270 --> 01:08:50.930
HAGEGE: New?

01:08:50.930 --> 01:08:51.930
What do you mean by new?

01:08:51.930 --> 01:08:53.222
Which ones?

01:08:53.222 --> 01:08:57.110
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] that
communicate with [INAUDIBLE]

01:08:57.110 --> 01:08:58.082
developed its own code.

01:08:58.082 --> 01:09:00.512
And with the speed
of the internet,

01:09:00.512 --> 01:09:03.136
this process is really fast.

01:09:03.136 --> 01:09:04.886
So maybe you observe
new languages getting

01:09:04.886 --> 01:09:07.819
born every day on the internet.

01:09:07.819 --> 01:09:10.586
HAGEGE: You mean new human
languages or made up languages?

01:09:10.586 --> 01:09:11.960
AUDIENCE: Made up by users.

01:09:11.960 --> 01:09:13.880
HAGEGE: Yeah, but
excuse me, excuse me,

01:09:13.880 --> 01:09:15.410
I am a professional linguist.

01:09:15.410 --> 01:09:18.979
I would not call these way
of communications, languages.

01:09:18.979 --> 01:09:20.990
A language, for me,
is what you know.

01:09:20.990 --> 01:09:22.189
It has a history.

01:09:22.189 --> 01:09:23.580
It has a grammar.

01:09:23.580 --> 01:09:26.420
It is not exclusively
in written form

01:09:26.420 --> 01:09:30.050
on the screen or your
internet of your computer.

01:09:30.050 --> 01:09:31.460
Its language we've spoken.

01:09:31.460 --> 01:09:35.540
So the codes, I would call
them codes, which appear

01:09:35.540 --> 01:09:36.899
and which are very numerous.

01:09:36.899 --> 01:09:40.430
They invade, in a way,
internet, do not disturb me.

01:09:40.430 --> 01:09:41.600
They are not languages.

01:09:41.600 --> 01:09:44.510
They are codes, or
means of communicating,

01:09:44.510 --> 01:09:47.800
which of course probably are
very useful to many people who

01:09:47.800 --> 01:09:48.420
use them.

01:09:48.420 --> 01:09:53.080
But I would not study them
within my work as a linguist.

01:09:53.080 --> 01:09:54.038
Yes?

01:09:54.038 --> 01:09:55.204
AUDIENCE: I have a question.

01:09:55.204 --> 01:09:57.165
At the beginning of
the talk, am I correct

01:09:57.165 --> 01:10:01.110
in understand that you said
that languages, plural, are

01:10:01.110 --> 01:10:05.090
associated with the
beginning of settled

01:10:05.090 --> 01:10:06.090
communities [INAUDIBLE]?

01:10:06.090 --> 01:10:07.298
HAGEGE: Associated with what?

01:10:07.298 --> 01:10:11.510
AUDIENCE: Of settled communities
and the construction of cities.

01:10:11.510 --> 01:10:15.941
Did I hear that correctly?

01:10:15.941 --> 01:10:17.690
HAGEGE: Well I say--
yes, you are correct.

01:10:17.690 --> 01:10:19.832
AUDIENCE: Can you repeat
the question please?

01:10:19.832 --> 01:10:21.540
HAGEGE: He's quite
correct, what he said.

01:10:21.540 --> 01:10:22.860
He caught what I meant.

01:10:22.860 --> 01:10:23.998
Yes.

01:10:23.998 --> 01:10:27.211
AUDIENCE: I didn't hear it.

01:10:27.211 --> 01:10:28.359
There was a question.

01:10:28.359 --> 01:10:29.400
HAGEGE: It was a comment.

01:10:37.250 --> 01:10:39.250
AUDIENCE: If I was
to describe how

01:10:39.250 --> 01:10:44.154
do you reconcile this with
information that came later

01:10:44.154 --> 01:10:47.398
in your talk concerning
the arrival of Europeans

01:10:47.398 --> 01:10:51.889
in North America who found
hundred of languages,

01:10:51.889 --> 01:10:54.883
hundreds of communities
spoke different languages,

01:10:54.883 --> 01:10:57.900
did not understand
each other at all.

01:10:57.900 --> 01:10:59.322
HAGEGE: To some
extent, they did.

01:10:59.322 --> 01:11:00.780
AUDIENCE: Many of
these communities

01:11:00.780 --> 01:11:02.640
were hunters and gatherers.

01:11:02.640 --> 01:11:05.328
We have the same kind of
situation in the Amazon

01:11:05.328 --> 01:11:08.196
where we have hunting and
gathering societies presumably.

01:11:08.196 --> 01:11:10.395
And communities that
live within a kilometer

01:11:10.395 --> 01:11:13.105
of each other and
they do not understand

01:11:13.105 --> 01:11:16.051
each other's language.

01:11:16.051 --> 01:11:18.788
So I wonder where this
argument comes from

01:11:18.788 --> 01:11:24.222
and why it is going to be still
here, this kind of argument

01:11:24.222 --> 01:11:29.656
being wielded after
2,000 years hearing it,

01:11:29.656 --> 01:11:35.920
for instance, in Greek writing,
the notion that those who spoke

01:11:35.920 --> 01:11:38.577
[INAUDIBLE] who
lived in the cities,

01:11:38.577 --> 01:11:42.136
and those who didn't were
the [INAUDIBLE] who barked,

01:11:42.136 --> 01:11:43.530
in a sense.

01:11:43.530 --> 01:11:45.202
HAGEGE: They don't bark.

01:11:45.202 --> 01:11:47.166
AUDIENCE: At the
people in the cities.

01:11:47.166 --> 01:11:50.970
So I'm wondering about the
status of this argument.

01:11:50.970 --> 01:11:54.457
HAGEGE: Well, there is a piece
of information which is lacking

01:11:54.457 --> 01:11:56.040
and which I would
like to provide you.

01:11:56.040 --> 01:11:59.400
In fact, these people had what
we call the vehicular languages

01:11:59.400 --> 01:12:02.190
in the Amazon, in
the Great Plains

01:12:02.190 --> 01:12:04.230
among the Algonquins,
Iroquoians and so on.

01:12:04.230 --> 01:12:06.580
There were many
vehicular languages.

01:12:06.580 --> 01:12:08.430
I mean, languages
whose origin was

01:12:08.430 --> 01:12:10.980
taken from one of the tribes
and which imposed themselves

01:12:10.980 --> 01:12:13.170
on all of the tribes
and they were used

01:12:13.170 --> 01:12:14.820
as communication languages.

01:12:14.820 --> 01:12:17.490
This is what the whites
found when they arrived.

01:12:17.490 --> 01:12:20.100
In all these contexts,
we had-- they had been

01:12:20.100 --> 01:12:21.560
described by some missionaries.

01:12:21.560 --> 01:12:26.100
And we have some elements
of information on them,

01:12:26.100 --> 01:12:30.090
in all these communities,
which where, it is true,

01:12:30.090 --> 01:12:33.900
split up into many, many tribes.

01:12:33.900 --> 01:12:36.480
They communicate with
each other through the use

01:12:36.480 --> 01:12:37.800
of these vehicular languages.

01:12:37.800 --> 01:12:40.175
AUDIENCE: So this has nothing
to do with the construction

01:12:40.175 --> 01:12:41.349
of cities.

01:12:41.349 --> 01:12:42.640
HAGEGE: But this is much later.

01:12:42.640 --> 01:12:44.000
AUDIENCE: And specialization.

01:12:44.000 --> 01:12:46.085
HAGEGE: But this is much later.

01:12:46.085 --> 01:12:48.200
That was a kind of anachronism.

01:12:48.200 --> 01:12:50.620
The construction of
the cities goes back

01:12:50.620 --> 01:12:53.920
to a remote antiquity
which I would assign

01:12:53.920 --> 01:12:57.370
to 9,000 or 10,000 years
before the beginning

01:12:57.370 --> 01:13:00.760
of the Christian era,
where while the period you

01:13:00.760 --> 01:13:04.540
are referring to is the arrival
of the whites in America, which

01:13:04.540 --> 01:13:05.890
is the 16th century.

01:13:05.890 --> 01:13:11.170
Between the two, we
have 10,006 centuries.

01:13:11.170 --> 01:13:12.520
So it is not the same period.

01:13:12.520 --> 01:13:18.278
So an enormous lack of
time between the two.

01:13:18.278 --> 01:13:23.218
AUDIENCE: But then
again [INAUDIBLE]

01:13:23.218 --> 01:13:25.194
contemporary societies,
quote unquote,

01:13:25.194 --> 01:13:30.134
"primitive," as examples
of where we come from.

01:13:30.134 --> 01:13:35.074
This is still
common in sciences.

01:13:35.074 --> 01:13:39.520
So that's why I'm asking about
the status of this argument

01:13:39.520 --> 01:13:42.292
because it sounds
like an argument that

01:13:42.292 --> 01:13:43.500
has been wielded for so long.

01:13:43.500 --> 01:13:45.430
HAGEGE: After?

01:13:45.430 --> 01:13:47.170
AUDIENCE: The argument
that essentially,

01:13:47.170 --> 01:13:50.118
the notion of the
diversification of languages

01:13:50.118 --> 01:13:53.760
is something that comes from the
appearance of settled society.

01:13:53.760 --> 01:13:56.235
HAGEGE: Yes.

01:13:56.235 --> 01:13:57.110
Do you agree with it?

01:13:57.110 --> 01:13:58.610
AUDIENCE: No, not at all.

01:13:58.610 --> 01:14:00.200
HAGEGE: What do you propose?

01:14:00.200 --> 01:14:02.180
AUDIENCE: I think
it's a fiction.

01:14:02.180 --> 01:14:03.739
HAGEGE: What do you propose?

01:14:03.739 --> 01:14:06.030
AUDIENCE: Well, I think that
the emergence of languages

01:14:06.030 --> 01:14:08.850
has to do with a new
[INAUDIBLE] which is not

01:14:08.850 --> 01:14:12.150
exclusive of settled societies.

01:14:12.150 --> 01:14:13.055
That's what my--

01:14:13.055 --> 01:14:13.930
HAGEGE: Well, no, no.

01:14:13.930 --> 01:14:14.680
What I say--

01:14:14.680 --> 01:14:17.050
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

01:14:17.050 --> 01:14:20.560
HAGEGE: What I say
is that we state

01:14:20.560 --> 01:14:24.460
something which can be
observed, that the increased

01:14:24.460 --> 01:14:26.710
number of languages
corresponds to the increased

01:14:26.710 --> 01:14:29.950
number of social relationships
is something which we know,

01:14:29.950 --> 01:14:31.450
which has been described.

01:14:31.450 --> 01:14:35.290
So of course, starting with
this point, which is recognized,

01:14:35.290 --> 01:14:37.660
agreed upon by
most scholars, what

01:14:37.660 --> 01:14:39.460
I say is to some
extent, hypothetical.

01:14:39.460 --> 01:14:44.980
It's true but I would not deny
many other linguists also have

01:14:44.980 --> 01:14:52.570
this view, claim that sedentary
life multiplied, increased,

01:14:52.570 --> 01:14:55.270
as a result, increase
the number of languages.

01:14:55.270 --> 01:14:59.770
Because languages
are a way by which

01:14:59.770 --> 01:15:03.430
human societies react to
the complexity of life

01:15:03.430 --> 01:15:04.540
in order to communicate.

01:15:04.540 --> 01:15:08.500
So it doesn't mean that
nomads had simple languages.

01:15:08.500 --> 01:15:09.850
They had fewer.

01:15:09.850 --> 01:15:11.860
But in fact, the
languages of Australia,

01:15:11.860 --> 01:15:15.640
which have been conserved,
and of Iroquois and Algonquin

01:15:15.640 --> 01:15:20.470
tribes in America, going back
to a very old period of time,

01:15:20.470 --> 01:15:22.070
are very complicated.

01:15:22.070 --> 01:15:24.430
So what I say is
not that when you

01:15:24.430 --> 01:15:33.490
have hunter-gatherer
societies becoming sedentary,

01:15:33.490 --> 01:15:35.920
they have more
complex languages,

01:15:35.920 --> 01:15:38.260
they have many more
languages, I say,

01:15:38.260 --> 01:15:41.770
which are not
necessarily more complex.

01:15:41.770 --> 01:15:44.560
But they multiply the
number because themselves

01:15:44.560 --> 01:15:47.960
multiply the settlements and
the circumstances in which they

01:15:47.960 --> 01:15:48.460
live.

01:15:48.460 --> 01:15:49.750
This is what I say.

01:15:49.750 --> 01:15:51.900
To some extent, I repeat,
it's hypothetical.

01:15:51.900 --> 01:15:56.570
So you justified to
put it into question.

01:15:56.570 --> 01:16:00.900
But it is an opinion which
is shared by many linguists,

01:16:00.900 --> 01:16:04.370
and not quite
demonstrable, I recognize.

01:16:04.370 --> 01:16:05.136
Yes?

01:16:05.136 --> 01:16:08.864
AUDIENCE: So how is that theory
[INAUDIBLE] with the fact that

01:16:08.864 --> 01:16:13.481
the linguistically most richest
areas of the world are areas

01:16:13.481 --> 01:16:18.640
where we have a lot of small,
isolated tribes that have

01:16:18.640 --> 01:16:19.419
trouble reaching--

01:16:19.419 --> 01:16:20.210
HAGEGE: What areas?

01:16:23.102 --> 01:16:24.999
AUDIENCE: The
Australian Aborigines--

01:16:24.999 --> 01:16:25.790
HAGEGE: New Guinea.

01:16:25.790 --> 01:16:28.430
AUDIENCE: Sub-Saharan
Africa, and New Guinea.

01:16:28.430 --> 01:16:29.350
HAGEGE: Yes.

01:16:29.350 --> 01:16:32.630
AUDIENCE: Whereas in the
more industrial areas, where

01:16:32.630 --> 01:16:34.735
the communication
is more enhanced

01:16:34.735 --> 01:16:38.818
and you have the need to talk
and relate, and communicate

01:16:38.818 --> 01:16:41.985
with much more people,
the number of languages

01:16:41.985 --> 01:16:43.720
is of course--

01:16:43.720 --> 01:16:44.720
HAGEGE: Is much smaller.

01:16:44.720 --> 01:16:45.303
AUDIENCE: Yes.

01:16:45.303 --> 01:16:46.716
HAGEGE: What's your question?

01:16:46.716 --> 01:16:49.644
AUDIENCE: Well, how does that
reconcile with the view that

01:16:49.644 --> 01:16:52.572
more cities and
more settlements--

01:16:52.572 --> 01:16:56.000
that the emergence of
cities and settlements--

01:16:56.000 --> 01:16:58.880
HAGEGE: Oh, it's very
easy to reconcile.

01:16:58.880 --> 01:17:01.460
I would say for
ecological reasons

01:17:01.460 --> 01:17:04.467
because as you may know, the
part of the world in which we

01:17:04.467 --> 01:17:06.800
have many, many tribes picking
many different languages,

01:17:06.800 --> 01:17:11.180
diverging languages,
which do not allow--

01:17:11.180 --> 01:17:13.940
despite the existence
of vehicular languages--

01:17:13.940 --> 01:17:17.270
an easy communication, all of
these parts of the world, New

01:17:17.270 --> 01:17:21.440
Guinea, northern territories
of Australia, and so on,

01:17:21.440 --> 01:17:25.370
are very mountainous
places in which people--

01:17:25.370 --> 01:17:27.380
the Caucasus also--
in which people

01:17:27.380 --> 01:17:33.100
are separated by relief
accidents, which are very high.

01:17:33.100 --> 01:17:36.050
So the way
socio-linguistics tries

01:17:36.050 --> 01:17:39.860
to explain, its proposal, of
course, which can be improved,

01:17:39.860 --> 01:17:43.250
the way social linguists try
to explain this situation,

01:17:43.250 --> 01:17:45.380
as being non-contradictory,
is that this

01:17:45.380 --> 01:17:51.470
is related to the
geographical configurations

01:17:51.470 --> 01:17:52.700
of these countries.

01:17:52.700 --> 01:17:53.540
And we know--

01:17:53.540 --> 01:17:55.370
I have been myself
to New Guinea.

01:17:55.370 --> 01:17:59.030
We have a central chain, which
is extremely inaccessible

01:17:59.030 --> 01:18:04.860
and in which it turns out that
it is the very one in which we

01:18:04.860 --> 01:18:08.640
have the most, probably the most
important number of languages

01:18:08.640 --> 01:18:10.050
in the world in New Guinea.

01:18:10.050 --> 01:18:12.240
And the other one is Caucuses
for the same reasons.

01:18:12.240 --> 01:18:15.930
So these people, of
course, are sedentary.

01:18:15.930 --> 01:18:17.490
They are in cities.

01:18:17.490 --> 01:18:22.050
They are-- they live more or
less in societies, which do not

01:18:22.050 --> 01:18:24.484
numerize any longer
and have not done it

01:18:24.484 --> 01:18:25.650
for centuries and centuries.

01:18:25.650 --> 01:18:28.530
But they are split up
in many, many languages

01:18:28.530 --> 01:18:31.770
because the communication
is made difficult by--

01:18:31.770 --> 01:18:35.230
as opposed to that we know,
we have the opposite example.

01:18:35.230 --> 01:18:38.520
We know that islands which
have no mountains, which

01:18:38.520 --> 01:18:49.320
are quite flat like Samoa,
like New Caledonia, like Tonga,

01:18:49.320 --> 01:18:51.540
have few languages, very few.

01:18:51.540 --> 01:18:53.520
And since these
people, even when

01:18:53.520 --> 01:18:58.200
there was a great distance
of marine territory,

01:18:58.200 --> 01:19:01.440
these people were navigators
and were always related

01:19:01.440 --> 01:19:04.450
to do commerce between them.

01:19:04.450 --> 01:19:07.230
These are places in which
we have very few languages.

01:19:07.230 --> 01:19:08.460
There is one Tongan language.

01:19:08.460 --> 01:19:11.640
There is one Fijian,
Fiji, and so on.

01:19:11.640 --> 01:19:14.910
As opposed to Caucuses, New
Guinea, and other mountainous

01:19:14.910 --> 01:19:19.080
part of the world, which are
a proliferation of languages.

01:19:19.080 --> 01:19:24.870
This, for me, is precisely
a social linguistics itself.

01:19:24.870 --> 01:19:27.120
We explain language
differentiation

01:19:27.120 --> 01:19:33.290
and multiplication by
ecological conditions.

01:19:33.290 --> 01:19:35.020
It is a way we
try to explain it.

01:19:35.020 --> 01:19:36.570
Yes?

01:19:36.570 --> 01:19:38.910
AUDIENCE: I would like to
ask about the relationship

01:19:38.910 --> 01:19:43.450
between the language
loss and cultural loss.

01:19:43.450 --> 01:19:47.900
And I think if we
think of language

01:19:47.900 --> 01:19:52.550
as a repertoire of cultural
notions and values,

01:19:52.550 --> 01:19:55.960
and also a focus of
culture in itself,

01:19:55.960 --> 01:19:58.640
in its aesthetic use
of one's own language,

01:19:58.640 --> 01:20:02.480
it's self conscious and
within culture, that's

01:20:02.480 --> 01:20:05.060
why we feel that
the loss of language

01:20:05.060 --> 01:20:09.840
is tragic because it
represents the loss of culture.

01:20:09.840 --> 01:20:11.550
However, it seems to
me there is a case

01:20:11.550 --> 01:20:15.570
to be made that language is
sort of flexible over culture,

01:20:15.570 --> 01:20:17.930
that they don't coincide.

01:20:17.930 --> 01:20:20.050
They're not homologous.

01:20:20.050 --> 01:20:23.510
There are cases of obliteration,
the American Indian case,

01:20:23.510 --> 01:20:28.840
where language and culture
loss are sort of coincidental.

01:20:28.840 --> 01:20:32.810
You get obliteration of culture
followed by language loss.

01:20:32.810 --> 01:20:34.910
But there are also many cases--

01:20:34.910 --> 01:20:36.680
and I think some of
the evidence for this

01:20:36.680 --> 01:20:39.320
is linguistic areas or
cultural areas where

01:20:39.320 --> 01:20:43.400
you get cultural
forms that include

01:20:43.400 --> 01:20:47.990
lexical fields, grammatical
forms, and even aesthetic forms

01:20:47.990 --> 01:20:51.590
like poetic genre that spread
across language boundaries.

01:20:51.590 --> 01:20:55.610
Southeast Asia is of great
cultural convergent center,

01:20:55.610 --> 01:20:58.280
for example, where all the
languages like Vietnamese,

01:20:58.280 --> 01:21:02.270
Thai, Chinese, and so on,
all have common features,

01:21:02.270 --> 01:21:06.110
suggesting that somehow these
features that we associate

01:21:06.110 --> 01:21:08.360
with particular cultures are
spreading across language

01:21:08.360 --> 01:21:09.200
lines.

01:21:09.200 --> 01:21:12.650
The Hebrew case you mentioned,
is another interesting case

01:21:12.650 --> 01:21:15.890
where Hebrew was
replaced by Aramaic

01:21:15.890 --> 01:21:17.630
and then replaced
by Hebrew again,

01:21:17.630 --> 01:21:21.830
which suggests that something
survive through this.

01:21:21.830 --> 01:21:24.490
And I'm wondering
if indeed we have--

01:21:24.490 --> 01:21:26.390
and you might use
English as an example,

01:21:26.390 --> 01:21:29.150
in which the various
Englishes of the world

01:21:29.150 --> 01:21:31.430
seem to, for
instance, Singaporean,

01:21:31.430 --> 01:21:33.560
which is a kind of
Creole English, I guess.

01:21:33.560 --> 01:21:38.420
Seems to preserve within it
many of the things that--

01:21:38.420 --> 01:21:40.680
many of the notions,
the semantic notions,

01:21:40.680 --> 01:21:44.929
the cultural notions that
survive out of Malay, Chinese,

01:21:44.929 --> 01:21:46.970
and the other components
that went into Singapore

01:21:46.970 --> 01:21:47.870
[INAUDIBLE].

01:21:47.870 --> 01:21:52.400
So is it the case that you
have, sort of, various kinds

01:21:52.400 --> 01:21:53.670
of language death?

01:21:53.670 --> 01:21:55.550
That you have the
tragic kind of death,

01:21:55.550 --> 01:21:57.179
which eliminates
culture with it.

01:21:57.179 --> 01:21:58.970
But you have other
kinds where we might not

01:21:58.970 --> 01:22:00.530
feel that it's quite so tragic.

01:22:00.530 --> 01:22:02.870
In fact, we might not
want to call it death.

01:22:02.870 --> 01:22:05.420
That what we feel is
so valuable somehow

01:22:05.420 --> 01:22:08.242
survives in the new language.

01:22:08.242 --> 01:22:10.450
HAGEGE: Well, there are many
things mixed up together

01:22:10.450 --> 01:22:13.240
in what you say.

01:22:13.240 --> 01:22:16.840
First of all, I will--

01:22:16.840 --> 01:22:19.360
by the way, if I may ask,
will you be here tomorrow?

01:22:19.360 --> 01:22:23.590
Tomorrow I will give a talk in
which I will mention Englishes.

01:22:23.590 --> 01:22:27.580
And it will be due
to this very subject,

01:22:27.580 --> 01:22:31.707
to what extent English
and other languages.

01:22:31.707 --> 01:22:33.040
I will talk about that in this--

01:22:33.040 --> 01:22:35.060
I am very interested
in this subject.

01:22:35.060 --> 01:22:38.380
However, in what you say,
when you started speaking,

01:22:38.380 --> 01:22:40.480
I wondered whether
you were going

01:22:40.480 --> 01:22:44.500
to say that if languages appear,
culture does not disappear.

01:22:44.500 --> 01:22:46.840
But you switch it
to another subject.

01:22:46.840 --> 01:22:50.260
And this is the beginning
of your question.

01:22:50.260 --> 01:22:54.100
And I quite agree that
language death, when

01:22:54.100 --> 01:22:57.040
it is the loss of a
language by population,

01:22:57.040 --> 01:22:59.770
does not imply necessarily
cultural death.

01:22:59.770 --> 01:23:02.020
I thought you were
going to mention

01:23:02.020 --> 01:23:05.050
gastronomy, dancing, poetry.

01:23:05.050 --> 01:23:08.500
There are many cultural
forms which belong in culture

01:23:08.500 --> 01:23:10.120
but we do not coincide
with language.

01:23:10.120 --> 01:23:13.360
And in this country, we
know, that the only ones who

01:23:13.360 --> 01:23:16.900
are able to cook things
which we can taste

01:23:16.900 --> 01:23:21.280
are people coming from
old countries in which one

01:23:21.280 --> 01:23:24.460
knew how to cook food.

01:23:24.460 --> 01:23:27.970
And in that case, I
must say, that even

01:23:27.970 --> 01:23:31.510
in those cases in which
people lose their languages

01:23:31.510 --> 01:23:34.469
to English, they do not lose
other parts of their cultures,

01:23:34.469 --> 01:23:36.010
including gastronomy,
which you know,

01:23:36.010 --> 01:23:39.430
French people are
very interested in.

01:23:39.430 --> 01:23:41.699
But you then switch
it to another aspect

01:23:41.699 --> 01:23:43.240
which is also very
interesting, which

01:23:43.240 --> 01:23:47.060
has, excuse me, nothing to do
with what you started with,

01:23:47.060 --> 01:23:51.520
which is borrowing
between languages,

01:23:51.520 --> 01:23:52.690
transmitting by borrowing.

01:23:52.690 --> 01:23:54.400
This is quite true.

01:23:54.400 --> 01:23:55.780
I quite agree.

01:23:55.780 --> 01:23:58.090
You mentioned southeast
Asian languages,

01:23:58.090 --> 01:24:05.470
Thai, Khmer, Burmese, Chinese
are languages whose bearers,

01:24:05.470 --> 01:24:08.589
whose speakers were
always in relationship

01:24:08.589 --> 01:24:09.880
during centuries and centuries.

01:24:09.880 --> 01:24:13.510
And of course, we know
Vietnamese is it mixing up

01:24:13.510 --> 01:24:14.470
of Thai and Chinese.

01:24:14.470 --> 01:24:17.770
It is a Creole as Thai with
many Chinese borrowing.

01:24:17.770 --> 01:24:19.750
I myself, I taught
Chinese very long.

01:24:19.750 --> 01:24:24.610
And I lived in China
during years and years.

01:24:24.610 --> 01:24:26.170
And of course,
during this time, I

01:24:26.170 --> 01:24:31.750
was a so-called
senior Tibetanist,

01:24:31.750 --> 01:24:34.060
or Tibet or Burma [INAUDIBLE].

01:24:34.060 --> 01:24:36.950
And I studied this
aspect of the things.

01:24:36.950 --> 01:24:43.960
And what I saw, I can say, is
that then, as you yourself say,

01:24:43.960 --> 01:24:48.190
these languages constituted and
still go on, considering today

01:24:48.190 --> 01:24:53.860
a kind of set between the
parts of which were saturated

01:24:53.860 --> 01:24:56.201
and where borrowed or
given back, and so on.

01:24:56.201 --> 01:24:57.700
As far as Hebrew
is concerned, which

01:24:57.700 --> 01:25:00.550
is a third part of
your small talk,

01:25:00.550 --> 01:25:11.980
I would say, speaking Hebrew
as the Jews did, up to or down

01:25:11.980 --> 01:25:16.480
to the Babylon exile,
then switching to Aramean,

01:25:16.480 --> 01:25:23.110
then reviving Hebrew in the
first half of the 20th century

01:25:23.110 --> 01:25:26.260
is a special case of
language death and language

01:25:26.260 --> 01:25:27.130
resurrection.

01:25:27.130 --> 01:25:32.420
But if the core of
your argument is

01:25:32.420 --> 01:25:35.730
that there are various types of
language death, I quite agree.

01:25:35.730 --> 01:25:36.630
It's true.

01:25:36.630 --> 01:25:38.440
And if it is-- it was
not clear in my talk,

01:25:38.440 --> 01:25:40.231
it is because my talk
was bad because it is

01:25:40.231 --> 01:25:42.230
exactly what I wanted to say.

01:25:42.230 --> 01:25:45.180
I quite agree that we have
various versions of language

01:25:45.180 --> 01:25:45.680
death.

01:25:45.680 --> 01:25:47.291
This is quite true.

01:25:47.291 --> 01:25:48.290
And thank you very much.

01:25:48.290 --> 01:25:49.456
AUDIENCE: One last question?

01:25:49.456 --> 01:25:50.738
HAGEGE: Another question?

01:25:50.738 --> 01:25:52.797
AUDIENCE: I wonder
[INAUDIBLE] European,

01:25:52.797 --> 01:25:54.380
do you think that
European integration

01:25:54.380 --> 01:25:57.509
will be good or bad for
minority languages in Europe.

01:25:57.509 --> 01:25:58.300
Some people think--

01:25:58.300 --> 01:25:59.710
HAGEGE: European expression?

01:25:59.710 --> 01:26:01.001
AUDIENCE: European integration.

01:26:01.001 --> 01:26:01.940
You know, with the EU.

01:26:01.940 --> 01:26:03.720
HAGEGE: In what
meaning, integration?

01:26:03.720 --> 01:26:06.660
AUDIENCE: Well, perhaps
the clinical integration,

01:26:06.660 --> 01:26:09.730
you know, perhaps as the nation
states become less important

01:26:09.730 --> 01:26:11.490
relative to Brussels,
it will be less

01:26:11.490 --> 01:26:15.640
important to speak Spanish as
opposed to Catalan or English

01:26:15.640 --> 01:26:18.653
as opposed to Welsh?

01:26:18.653 --> 01:26:19.861
HAGEGE: What's your question?

01:26:19.861 --> 01:26:23.629
AUDIENCE: Well, do you think
that we'll see a boost--

01:26:23.629 --> 01:26:27.222
or we'll see good prospects for
survival and growth of minority

01:26:27.222 --> 01:26:28.198
languages in Europe?

01:26:28.198 --> 01:26:30.638
Or do you think that
they're on the way out

01:26:30.638 --> 01:26:33.580
and everyone will be speaking
a few central languages?

01:26:33.580 --> 01:26:36.180
HAGEGE: You know, I
am a language lover.

01:26:36.180 --> 01:26:41.120
And a minority languages I love
as much as any other languages.

01:26:41.120 --> 01:26:46.750
So Catalan, Basque,
what's the name

01:26:46.750 --> 01:26:49.974
of this language in
the Northwest of Spain.

01:26:49.974 --> 01:26:52.720
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

01:26:52.720 --> 01:27:04.750
HAGEGE: [SPEAKING SPANISH] So
therefore, these languages,

01:27:04.750 --> 01:27:08.000
of course, I am
for their defense.

01:27:08.000 --> 01:27:11.860
And I don't think
their fate is so dim.

01:27:11.860 --> 01:27:16.130
It might be a brilliant future
which is in front of them.

01:27:16.130 --> 01:27:16.870
Why?

01:27:16.870 --> 01:27:21.010
Because as you
may know, the ones

01:27:21.010 --> 01:27:23.860
who promote these
languages have understood

01:27:23.860 --> 01:27:26.460
that what the central
government refused,

01:27:26.460 --> 01:27:30.490
Brussels and unified
Europe can accept.

01:27:30.490 --> 01:27:34.660
And this is reason why most
of the defenders and promoters

01:27:34.660 --> 01:27:37.720
of these regional
languages, they

01:27:37.720 --> 01:27:41.050
are called regional languages,
address themselves to Brussels

01:27:41.050 --> 01:27:45.880
when they don't obtain a
fulfillment of their requests

01:27:45.880 --> 01:27:49.120
from Paris, Madrid, or Rome.

01:27:49.120 --> 01:27:52.990
So I think that the future of
regional language in Europe

01:27:52.990 --> 01:27:55.450
is by no means threatened
as much, probably,

01:27:55.450 --> 01:27:58.000
as the one of the great
languages themselves.

